var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"dftk/#DFTK.jl-Integration","page":"DFTK.jl Integration","text":"This is a variant of the API Workflow that enables a complete Julia-only workflow from first-principles electronic structure calculations to Boltzmann transport coefficients.\n\nBy combining DFTK.jl (Julia-native DFT) with BoltzTraP.jl, the entire workflow runs in a single Julia session without external dependencies.\n\n","title":"DFTK.jl Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"dftk/#Workflow","page":"DFTK.jl Integration","text":"The input is the SCF result (scfres) from DFTK's self_consistent_field function.\n\nDFTK.jl                    BoltzTraP.jl\n┌──────────┐              ┌──────────────┐\n│ scfres   │──────────────│ load_dftk    │\n│ (bands,  │   Extension  │ (extract)    │\n│  kpts)   │              └──────┬───────┘\n└──────────┘                     │\n                                 ▼\n                    ┌────────────────────┐\n                    │ run_interpolate    │\n                    └────────┬───────────┘\n                             ▼\n                    ┌────────────────────┐\n                    │ run_integrate      │\n                    └────────┬───────────┘\n                             ▼\n                    ┌────────────────────┐\n                    │ TransportResult    │\n                    └────────────────────┘","title":"Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"dftk/#Example","page":"DFTK.jl Integration","text":"using DFTK\nusing BoltzTraP\n\n# Setup DFTK calculation\na = 10.26  # Silicon lattice constant (Bohr)\nlattice = a / 2 * [[0 1 1.]; [1 0 1.]; [1 1 0.]]\nSi = ElementPsp(:Si; psp=load_psp(\"hgh/lda/si-q4\"))\natoms = [Si, Si]\npositions = [ones(3)/8, -ones(3)/8]\n\nmodel = model_LDA(lattice, atoms, positions)\nbasis = PlaneWaveBasis(model; Ecut=15, kgrid=[10, 10, 10])\nscfres = self_consistent_field(basis)\n\n# Extract data for BoltzTraP\ndata = load_dftk(scfres)\n\n# Run interpolation and transport\ninterp = run_interpolate(data; source=\"DFTK\", kpoints=5000, verbose=true)\ntransport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures=[300.0])\n\n# Access results\nprintln(\"Seebeck at 300K: \", transport.seebeck[1, 1, 1, :])\n\nwarning: Load order\nusing DFTK must be called before using BoltzTraP to activate the extension.","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"dftk/#Notes","page":"DFTK.jl Integration","text":"DFTK uses atomic units (Hartree, Bohr) internally - no conversion needed\nDense k-grid (10x10x10 or more) recommended for accurate interpolation\nNon-spin-polarized and collinear magnetic calculations are supported (v0.3+)\n\n","title":"Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"dftk/#Testing","page":"DFTK.jl Integration","text":"BoltzTraP.jl uses Julia package extensions for optional dependencies. DFTK extension requires special handling when running tests.","title":"Testing"},{"category":"section","location":"dftk/#Unit-Tests","page":"DFTK.jl Integration","text":"Unit tests are in test/test_dftk_extension.jl:\n\n# DFTK tests are slow (require SCF), so they're gated by environment variable\nTEST_DFTK=true julia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'","title":"Unit Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"dftk/#Manual-Testing","page":"DFTK.jl Integration","text":"Test in REPL:\n\n# Must load DFTK BEFORE BoltzTraP to trigger extension\nusing DFTK\nusing BoltzTraP\n\n# Now load_dftk is available\n@assert hasmethod(load_dftk, Tuple{Any})","title":"Manual Testing"},{"category":"section","location":"dftk/#End-to-End-Test","page":"DFTK.jl Integration","text":"julia --project ftest/test_dftk_e2e.jl\n\nThis runs a complete workflow: DFTK SCF → load_dftk → run_interpolate → run_integrate.","title":"End-to-End Test"},{"category":"function","location":"dftk/#BoltzTraP.load_dftk","page":"DFTK.jl Integration","text":"load_dftk(scfres) -> DFTData{1}\n\nExtract band structure data from DFTK SCF result.\n\nThis function requires DFTK.jl to be loaded. Load it with:\n\nusing DFTK\nusing BoltzTraP\n\nSee the DFTK extension documentation for details.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.load_dftk"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#utilities","page":"Utilities","text":"Additional functions for band structure analysis and transport calculations.\n\n","title":"Utility Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Electronic-Heat-Capacity","page":"Utilities","text":"","title":"Electronic Heat Capacity"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#calc_cv","page":"Utilities","text":"Compute the electronic contribution to the heat capacity.","title":"calc_cv"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Example","page":"Utilities","text":"using BoltzTraP\n\n# Load interpolation result\ninterp = load_interpolation(\"si_interp.jld2\")\n\n# Get DOS from interpolation\nepsilon = interp.epsilon\ndos = interp.dos\n\n# Define chemical potential and temperature ranges\nmu_range = range(-0.1, 0.1, length=101)  # Ha\nT_range = [100.0, 200.0, 300.0, 400.0, 500.0]  # K\n\n# Calculate electronic heat capacity\ncv = calc_cv(epsilon, dos, mu_range, T_range; dosweight=2.0)\n# Returns: (nT, nμ) matrix in J/K","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Using-with-TransportResult-(Recommended)","page":"Utilities","text":"using BoltzTraP\n\n# Run transport calculation\ntransport = run_integrate(\"si_interp.jld2\"; temperatures=[300.0])\n\n# Convenience method - extracts all parameters automatically\ncv = calc_cv(transport)\n\nAll required parameters (epsilon, dos, mu_values, temperatures, dosweight) are automatically extracted from the TransportResult.","title":"Using with TransportResult (Recommended)"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Output","page":"Utilities","text":"Field Type Description\ncv Matrix{Float64} Heat capacity (nT × nμ) in J/K\n\n","title":"Output"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Scissor-Correction","page":"Utilities","text":"","title":"Scissor Correction"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#apply_scissor","page":"Utilities","text":"Apply a scissor correction to shift conduction bands relative to valence bands.","title":"apply_scissor"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Use-Case","page":"Utilities","text":"DFT calculations often underestimate band gaps. The scissor correction shifts conduction band energies by a constant value to match experimental band gaps.","title":"Use Case"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#High-Level-API-(Recommended)","page":"Utilities","text":"Use the scissor parameter in run_integrate:\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\n# Apply scissor correction to achieve 1.1 eV band gap\ntransport = run_integrate(\"si_interp.jld2\";\n    temperatures = [300.0],\n    scissor = 1.1  # Target gap in eV\n)\n\n# Check if scissor was applied\nif haskey(transport.metadata, \"scissor_eV\")\n    println(\"Scissor applied: $(transport.metadata[\"scissor_eV\"]) eV\")\nend","title":"High-Level API (Recommended)"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#CLI-Usage","page":"Utilities","text":"boltztrap integrate si_interp.jld2 -t 300 --scissor 1.1","title":"CLI Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Low-Level-API","page":"Utilities","text":"For direct manipulation of band energies:\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\n# Apply scissor to raw band data\neband_shifted = apply_scissor(epsilon, dos, nelect, eband, desired_gap;\n    dosweight = 2.0\n)\n\nWhere desired_gap is in Hartree. Use EV_TO_HA for conversion from eV.\n\n","title":"Low-Level API"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#DOS-Smoothing-(v0.3)","page":"Utilities","text":"","title":"DOS Smoothing (v0.3)"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#smoothen_DOS","page":"Utilities","text":"note: Coming in v0.3\nThis function will be available in v0.3.\n\nApply Gaussian smoothing to the density of states.\n\n","title":"smoothen_DOS"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Validation","page":"Utilities","text":"To validate calc_cv against Python BoltzTraP2:\n\njulia --project validation/compare_cv.jl\n\nSee Validation for details.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"function","location":"utilities/#BoltzTraP.calc_cv","page":"Utilities","text":"calc_cv(epsilon, dos, μ_range, T_range; dosweight=2.0) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nCompute the electronic contribution to the heat capacity.\n\nArguments\n\nepsilon: energy grid (npts,), in Hartree\ndos: density of states (npts,), from BTPDOS or TransportResult.dos\nμ_range: chemical potential values (nμ,), in Hartree\nT_range: temperature values (nT,), in Kelvin\ndosweight: spin degeneracy (2.0 for unpolarized, 1.0 for polarized)\n\nReturns\n\nMatrix of shape (nT, nμ) with heat capacity in SI units (J/K).\n\nExample\n\n# From TransportResult\ntransport = run_integrate(\"si_interp.jld2\"; temperatures = [300.0])\ncv = calc_cv(transport.epsilon, transport.dos, transport.mu_values, transport.temperatures)\n\n\n\n\n\ncalc_cv(transport::TransportResult) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nCompute the electronic contribution to the heat capacity from transport results.\n\nThis is a convenience method that extracts all necessary data from the TransportResult struct. The epsilon, dos, temperatures, mu_values, and dosweight are automatically retrieved.\n\nReturns\n\nMatrix of shape (nT, nμ) with heat capacity in SI units (J/K).\n\nExample\n\ntransport = run_integrate(\"si_interp.jld2\"; temperatures = [300.0])\ncv = calc_cv(transport)\n\nSee also: calc_cv(epsilon, dos, μ_range, T_range; dosweight)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.calc_cv"},{"category":"function","location":"utilities/#BoltzTraP.apply_scissor","page":"Utilities","text":"apply_scissor(epsilon, dos, N0, eband, desired_gap; dosweight=2.0) -> Array\n\nShift all conduction bands to achieve the desired value of the band gap.\n\nThis function identifies bands that lie entirely above the Fermi level (conduction bands) and shifts them by a constant amount to match the specified band gap.\n\nArguments\n\nepsilon: energy grid from BTPDOS (npts,), in Hartree\ndos: density of states (npts,)\nN0: number of valence electrons\neband: band energies (nbands, nkpoints), in Hartree\ndesired_gap: target band gap in Hartree\ndosweight: maximum occupancy of an electron mode (2.0 for non-spin-polarized)\n\nReturns\n\nArray of shifted band energies with the same shape as eband.\n\nErrors\n\nArgumentError: if there is no gap at the Fermi level (material is metallic)\nArgumentError: if the Fermi energy lies outside the band energy range\n\nExample\n\n# Shift Si bands to achieve 1.1 eV gap\ndesired_gap = 1.1 * EV_TO_HA\neband_shifted = apply_scissor(epsilon, dos, nelect, eband, desired_gap)\n\nSee also: solve_for_mu\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.apply_scissor"},{"category":"section","location":"file_formats/#File-Formats","page":"File Formats","text":"BoltzTraP.jl uses specific file formats for input and output.","title":"File Formats"},{"category":"section","location":"file_formats/#Input-Formats","page":"File Formats","text":"","title":"Input Formats"},{"category":"section","location":"file_formats/#Format-Detection","page":"File Formats","text":"See detected_format for automatic format detection.\n\nExample:\n\nformat = detected_format(\"./calculation\")  # Returns \"VASP\", \"QE\", \"Wien2k\", \"GENE\", \"ABINIT\", or nothing","title":"Format Detection"},{"category":"section","location":"file_formats/#VASP","page":"File Formats","text":"BoltzTraP.jl reads VASP output files from a directory:\n\nFile Required Description\nvasprun.xml Yes Band energies, k-points, lattice, Fermi energy\nPOSCAR No Crystal structure (also in vasprun.xml)\nEIGENVAL No Alternative band energy source\n\nDirectory structure:\n\nSi.vasp/\n├── vasprun.xml    # Main data source\n├── POSCAR         # Optional\n└── EIGENVAL       # Optional","title":"VASP"},{"category":"section","location":"file_formats/#Quantum-ESPRESSO","page":"File Formats","text":"Reads Quantum ESPRESSO output:\n\nFile Description\n*.xml Data file from pw.x\nbands.dat.gnu Band data from bands.x\n\n","title":"Quantum ESPRESSO"},{"category":"section","location":"file_formats/#Output-Formats","page":"File Formats","text":"","title":"Output Formats"},{"category":"section","location":"file_formats/#Interpolation-Result-(.jld2)","page":"File Formats","text":"The interpolate command produces a JLD2 file containing:\n\nKey Type Description\ncoeffs Matrix{ComplexF64} Fourier coefficients (nbands x nequiv)\nequivalences Vector{Matrix{Int}} Equivalence class lattice points\nlattvec Matrix{Float64} Lattice vectors (3x3, Bohr)\nmetadata Dict{String,Any} See below\n\nMetadata contents:\n\nKey Type Description\nfermi_energy Float64 Fermi energy (Ha)\nnelect Float64 Number of electrons\ndosweight Float64 DOS weight (2.0 for non-spin-polarized)\nselected_bands UnitRange{Int} Band indices used\nsource_file String Original DFT file\ncreation_date String ISO 8601 timestamp\ngenerator String \"BoltzTraP.jl\"\n\nLoading in Julia:\n\nusing JLD2\n\ndata = load(\"interpolation.jld2\")\ncoeffs = data[\"coeffs\"]\nequivalences = data[\"equivalences\"]\nfermi = data[\"metadata\"][\"fermi_energy\"]","title":"Interpolation Result (.jld2)"},{"category":"section","location":"file_formats/#Transport-Result-(.jld2)","page":"File Formats","text":"The integrate command produces a JLD2 file containing:\n\nKey Type Description\ntemperatures Vector{Float64} Temperatures (K)\nmu Vector{Float64} Chemical potentials (Ha)\nN Matrix{Float64} Carrier concentration (nT x nmu)\nsigma Array{Float64,4} Conductivity/tau (3x3 x nT x nmu)\nseebeck Array{Float64,4} Seebeck coefficient (3x3 x nT x nmu)\nkappa Array{Float64,4} Thermal conductivity/tau (3x3 x nT x nmu)\nL0, L1, L2 Array{Float64,4} Fermi integrals\nmu0 Vector{Float64} Intrinsic chemical potential at each T\nepsilon Vector{Float64} DOS energy grid (Ha)\ndos Vector{Float64} Density of states\nvvdos Array{Float64,3} Velocity-velocity DOS\nmetadata Dict{String,Any} Source info, creation date\n\nLoading in Julia:\n\nusing JLD2\n\ntransport = load(\"transport.jld2\")\nsigma = transport[\"sigma\"]      # 3x3 x nT x nmu\nseebeck = transport[\"seebeck\"]  # 3x3 x nT x nmu","title":"Transport Result (.jld2)"},{"category":"section","location":"file_formats/#CSV-Export","page":"File Formats","text":"Transport results can be exported to CSV for external analysis:\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\ntransport = load_integrate(\"transport.jld2\")\nsave_integrate_csv(\"transport.csv\", transport)\n\nCSV columns:\n\nColumn Unit Description\nT K Temperature\nmu Ha Chemical potential\nN - Carrier concentration\nsigma_xx, sigma_yy, sigma_zz S/(m*s) Conductivity/tau diagonal\nS_xx, S_yy, S_zz V/K Seebeck coefficient diagonal\nkappa_xx, kappa_yy, kappa_zz W/(mKs) Thermal conductivity/tau diagonal\n\n","title":"CSV Export"},{"category":"section","location":"file_formats/#Compatibility-with-Python-BoltzTraP2","page":"File Formats","text":"BoltzTraP.jl supports reading Python BoltzTraP2's .bt2 interpolation files, enabling seamless migration from existing Python workflows.\n\nOperation Python .bt2 Julia .jld2\nRead interpolation ✅ Supported ✅ Native\nWrite interpolation ✅ Supported ✅ Native (default)\nRead transport ❌ Not supported ✅ Native\nWrite transport ❌ Not supported ✅ Native\n\nUsage examples:\n\n# Read Python .bt2 and compute transport\nboltztrap integrate python_result.bt2 -t 300\n\n# Describe .bt2 file contents\nboltztrap describe python_result.bt2\n\n# Plot bands from .bt2 (requires --kpath for files without spacegroup)\nboltztrap plotbands python_result.bt2 --kpath \"G:0,0,0;X:0.5,0,0.5|G-X-G\"\n\n# Write interpolation in .bt2 format\nboltztrap interpolate ./Si.vasp -o si_interp.bt2\n\nFormat details:\n\nFormat Structure Use case\n.bt2 LZMA-compressed JSON Python compatibility\n.jld2 HDF5-based binary Julia native (recommended)\n\nnote: Transport results\nTransport calculation results (.btj in Python) are only available in .jld2 format. This is intentional as Python BoltzTraP2 also uses plain text formats (.trace, .condtens) for transport output.\n\n","title":"Compatibility with Python BoltzTraP2"},{"category":"section","location":"file_formats/#File-Size-Guidelines","page":"File Formats","text":"Material k-points Interpolation Transport\nSi (2 atoms) 5000 ~1 MB ~5 MB\nComplex (50 atoms) 10000 ~50 MB ~20 MB\n\n","title":"File Size Guidelines"},{"category":"section","location":"file_formats/#Next-Steps","page":"File Formats","text":"Interpolation - InterpolationResult and save/load functions\nIntegration - TransportResult and save/load functions","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"magnetic/#Magnetic-Materials","page":"Magnetic Materials","text":"BoltzTraP.jl supports spin-polarized (collinear magnetic) calculations.","title":"Magnetic Materials"},{"category":"section","location":"magnetic/#Supported-Spin-Types","page":"Magnetic Materials","text":"Spin Type Support Notes\nNon-magnetic Full Default (dosweight=2)\nCollinear Full (v0.3+) Spin-polarized (dosweight=1)","title":"Supported Spin Types"},{"category":"section","location":"magnetic/#DFT-Calculation-Requirements","page":"Magnetic Materials","text":"To use collinear magnetic calculations, prepare spin-polarized DFT data:\n\nDFT Code Setting\nVASP ISPIN=2\nQuantum ESPRESSO nspin=2\nDFTK.jl spin_polarization=:collinear","title":"DFT Calculation Requirements"},{"category":"section","location":"magnetic/#Workflow","page":"Magnetic Materials","text":"No changes required - BoltzTraP.jl automatically detects spin polarization from the band structure dimensions.\n\nThe workflow is identical to non-magnetic calculations:\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\n# Load spin-polarized data (automatic detection)\ndata = load_vasp(\"./Fe.vasp\")\n\n# Run interpolation and transport (same API)\ninterp = run_interpolate(data; kpoints=5000)\ntransport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures=[300.0])","title":"Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"magnetic/#Verification","page":"Magnetic Materials","text":"You can verify that magnetic data was detected correctly:\n\ndata = load_vasp(\"./Fe.vasp\")\n\n# Check spin type\nis_magnetic(data)  # true\nnspin(data)        # 2","title":"Verification"},{"category":"section","location":"magnetic/#Result-Metadata","page":"Magnetic Materials","text":"TransportResult includes spin type information in its metadata field:\n\ntransport.metadata[\"spintype\"]   # \"Collinear\"\ntransport.metadata[\"dosweight\"]  # 1.0\n\nSee Output Formats for full TransportResult documentation.","title":"Result Metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"magnetic/#Internal-Handling","page":"Magnetic Materials","text":"When spin-polarized data is loaded, BoltzTraP.jl automatically:\n\nDetects spin polarization from band structure dimensions (nspin=2)\nConcatenates spin channels for Fourier interpolation (following Python BoltzTraP2)\nApplies time-reversal symmetry considerations for equivalence classes\nSets dosweight=1.0 (vs 2.0 for non-magnetic)","title":"Internal Handling"},{"category":"section","location":"magnetic/#Validation","page":"Magnetic Materials","text":"Fe collinear magnetic transport has been validated at T=1000K against Python BoltzTraP2.\n\nSee Validation for details.\n\nnote: Why T=1000K?\nAt lower temperatures (e.g., 300K), the Seebeck coefficient shows oscillations (~±250 μV/K) due to the BoltzTraP Fourier interpolation method. At T=1000K, thermal broadening naturally smooths these oscillations, enabling clean validation.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/#Performance-Benchmarks","page":"Benchmarks","text":"BoltzTraP.jl includes optimized implementations for computationally intensive functions. This page documents the performance improvements and shows how to reproduce the benchmarks.","title":"Performance Benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/#End-to-End-Performance-(vs-Python-BoltzTraP2)","page":"Benchmarks","text":"BoltzTraP.jl achieves 1.8-3.4x speedup over Python BoltzTraP2 for end-to-end transport calculations.\n\nHardware: MacBook Pro (Apple M2)\n\nk-points BoltzTraP2 (Python) BoltzTraP.jl Speedup\n1000 1.80 s 0.53 s 3.4x\n2000 2.48 s 1.14 s 2.2x\n4000 3.68 s 2.08 s 1.8x\n\nThe speedup is larger for smaller problems due to Julia's efficient compilation. For larger problems, both implementations become memory-bound with similar scaling.","title":"End-to-End Performance (vs Python BoltzTraP2)"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/#Running-Benchmarks","page":"Benchmarks","text":"Benchmark scripts are available in the benchmarks/ directory:\n\n# End-to-end scaling benchmarks (Python vs Julia)\npython benchmarks/scaling_benchmark.py   # Run Python BoltzTraP2\njulia --project benchmarks/scaling_benchmark.jl  # Run Julia BoltzTraP.jl\n\n# Generate comparison figure\njulia --project benchmarks/generate_figure.jl\n\nSee benchmarks/README.md for details.","title":"Running Benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Developer-Guide","page":"Developer Guide","text":"This page documents internal data structures, types, and workflows for developers extending BoltzTraP.jl.\n\nFor validating results against Python BoltzTraP2, see the Validation page.","title":"Developer Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Architecture-Overview","page":"Developer Guide","text":"┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                      Public API                              │\n│  run_interpolate, run_integrate, load_vasp, load_qe, ...    │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                              │\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    Workflow Layer                            │\n│  workflow.jl: orchestrates interpolation and integration    │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                              │\n┌──────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┬────────────────┐\n│   Symmetry   │ Interpolation│ Reconstruction│   Transport   │\n│  sphere.jl   │interpolation │reconstruction │  bandlib.jl   │\n│ symmetry.jl  │    .jl       │    .jl        │               │\n│equivalences.jl│             │               │               │\n└──────────────┴──────────────┴──────────────┴────────────────┘\n                              │\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                      I/O Layer                               │\n│  io/vasp.jl, io/qe.jl, io/serialization.jl, io/loader.jl   │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘","title":"Architecture Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Core-Data-Types","page":"Developer Guide","text":"","title":"Core Data Types"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#InterpolationResult","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Stores the result of band structure interpolation.\n\nstruct InterpolationResult\n    coeffs::Matrix{ComplexF64}       # Fourier coefficients (nbands × neq)\n    equivalences::Vector{Matrix{Int}} # Equivalence class representatives\n    lattvec::Matrix{Float64}         # Lattice vectors (3×3, Bohr)\n    atoms::Union{Nothing,Dict}       # Atomic structure (optional)\n    metadata::Dict{String,Any}       # Additional information\nend\n\nMetadata keys:\n\nfermi: Fermi energy (Ha)\nnelect: Number of electrons\ndosweight: DOS weight (2.0 for non-spin-polarized)\nselected_bands: Band indices used\nspacegroup_number, spacegroup_symbol: Space group info","title":"InterpolationResult"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#TransportResult","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Stores transport coefficients from BZ integration.\n\nstruct TransportResult\n    temperatures::Vector{Float64}    # Temperatures (K)\n    mu_values::Vector{Float64}       # Chemical potentials (eV)\n    sigma::Array{Float64,4}          # Conductivity (3×3×nT×nμ), S/m\n    seebeck::Array{Float64,4}        # Seebeck (3×3×nT×nμ), V/K\n    kappa::Array{Float64,4}          # Thermal conductivity (3×3×nT×nμ), W/(m·K)\n    dos::Union{Nothing,Dict}         # DOS data (optional)\n    metadata::Dict{String,Any}       # Additional information\nend","title":"TransportResult"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#FourierInterpolator","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Internal type for band interpolation (not exported).\n\nstruct FourierInterpolator\n    coeffs::Matrix{ComplexF64}       # Fourier coefficients\n    equivalences::Vector{Vector{SVector{3,Int}}}  # Equivalence classes\n    lattvec::SMatrix{3,3,Float64}    # Lattice vectors\nend\n\nKey methods:\n\ninterpolate_bands(interp, kpoints) → band energies\ninterpolate_velocities(interp, kpoints) → band velocities","title":"FourierInterpolator"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#I/O-Data-Format","page":"Developer Guide","text":"All loaders (load_vasp, load_qe, load_wien2k, load_gene, load_abinit, load_dftk) return a NamedTuple:\n\n(\n    lattice::Matrix{Float64},     # 3×3, Bohr\n    positions::Matrix{Float64},   # 3×natoms, fractional\n    species::Vector{String},      # Element symbols\n    kpoints::Matrix{Float64},     # 3×nkpts, fractional\n    weights::Vector{Float64},     # nkpts\n    ebands::Array{Float64,3},     # nbands×nkpts×nspin, Hartree\n    occupations::Array{Float64,3},# nbands×nkpts×nspin\n    fermi::Float64,               # Hartree\n    nelect::Float64,              # Number of electrons\n)","title":"I/O Data Format"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Internal-Functions","page":"Developer Guide","text":"","title":"Internal Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Sphere-Module-(sphere.jl)","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Function Description\ncompute_bounds(lattvec, radius) Compute search bounds for lattice points\ncompute_radius(lattvec, nrot, nkpt_target) Estimate radius for target k-points\ncalc_nrotations(lattvec, positions, types, magmom) Count symmetry operations\nget_unique_rotations(...) Get unique rotation matrices\ncalc_tensor_basis(...) Compute symmetry-adapted tensor basis","title":"Sphere Module (sphere.jl)"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Equivalences-Module-(equivalences.jl)","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Function Description\ncalc_sphere_quotient_set(...) Compute equivalence classes\nlattice_points_in_sphere(...) Find lattice points within radius","title":"Equivalences Module (equivalences.jl)"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Interpolation-Module-(interpolation.jl)","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Function Description\ncompute_phase_factors(kpoints, equivalences) Compute exp(2πi k·R)\nFourierInterpolator(kpts, ebands, equivs, lattvec) Fit Fourier coefficients\ninterpolate_bands(interp, kpoints) Evaluate bands at k-points\ninterpolate_velocities(interp, kpoints) Evaluate velocities","title":"Interpolation Module (interpolation.jl)"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Transport-Module-(bandlib.jl)","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Function Description\nfermi_dirac(ε, μ, kT) Fermi-Dirac distribution\ndfermi_dirac_de(ε, μ, kT) Derivative of f(E)\ncalc_Onsager_coefficients(...) Compute L0, L1, L2 tensors\ncalc_transport_coefficients(...) Compute σ, S, κ from Onsager\ncalc_N(...) Carrier concentration\nsolve_for_mu(...) Find μ for target carrier density","title":"Transport Module (bandlib.jl)"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Unit-Constants-(units.jl)","page":"Developer Guide","text":"const BOHR_TO_ANG = 0.529177210903     # Length conversion\nconst ANG_TO_BOHR = 1 / BOHR_TO_ANG\nconst HA_TO_EV = 27.211386245988       # Energy conversion\nconst EV_TO_HA = 1 / HA_TO_EV\nconst KB_AU = 3.1668115634438576e-6    # Boltzmann constant (Ha/K)\nconst SIGMA_CONV = 5.2586177987809155e-24   # σ/τ: S/(m·s)\nconst SEEBECK_CONV = 1.9325063968640632e-25 # S intermediate\nconst KAPPA_CONV = 7.101830018500863e-27    # κ/τ: W/(m·K·s)","title":"Unit Constants (units.jl)"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Adding-New-Features","page":"Developer Guide","text":"","title":"Adding New Features"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Adding-a-New-I/O-Format","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Create src/io/newformat.jl:\n\nfunction load_newformat(directory::String)\n    # Parse files and return NamedTuple\n    return (\n        lattice = ...,   # 3×3, Bohr\n        kpoints = ...,   # 3×nkpts, fractional\n        ebands = ...,    # nbands×nkpts×nspin, Hartree\n        fermi = ...,     # Hartree\n        # ... other fields\n    )\nend\n\nRegister in src/io/loader.jl:\n\nfunction _detect_newformat(directory::String)\n    isfile(joinpath(directory, \"newformat.out\"))\nend\n\n# In _init_loaders():\nregister_loader(\"NewFormat\", _detect_newformat, load_newformat)\n\nExport in src/BoltzTraP.jl:\n\nexport load_newformat","title":"Adding a New I/O Format"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Adding-a-New-Transport-Property","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Add calculation in src/bandlib.jl\nAdd to TransportResult struct in src/io/serialization.jl\nUpdate run_integrate in src/workflow.jl\nAdd CLI option in src/cli.jl (if needed)","title":"Adding a New Transport Property"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Debugging","page":"Developer Guide","text":"","title":"Debugging"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#CLI-Debug-Mode","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Use the --debug flag to enable detailed logging output:\n\n# Interpolation with debug output\nboltztrap interpolate ./Si.vasp --debug\n\n# Integration with debug output\nboltztrap integrate si_interp.jld2 -t 300 --debug\n\nDebug output includes:\n\nCLI argument values received\nDFT format detection process\nFile loading details\nWorkflow function parameters","title":"CLI Debug Mode"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Environment-Variable","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Alternatively, set the JULIA_DEBUG environment variable:\n\nJULIA_DEBUG=BoltzTraP boltztrap interpolate ./Si.vasp\n\nOr in Julia:\n\nENV[\"JULIA_DEBUG\"] = \"BoltzTraP\"\nusing BoltzTraP\n# Now all @debug statements will be printed","title":"Environment Variable"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Logging-Levels","page":"Developer Guide","text":"BoltzTraP.jl uses Julia's standard Logging module:\n\nLevel Purpose Enabled by\n@debug Internal details, argument values --debug flag\n@info Success messages Always\n@warn Non-fatal issues Always\n@error Fatal errors Always","title":"Logging Levels"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Verbose-Mode-vs-Debug-Mode","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Flag Output Audience\n-v, --verbose Progress messages via println End users\n--debug Internal state via @debug Developers\n\nExample with both:\n\nboltztrap interpolate ./Si.vasp -v --debug","title":"Verbose Mode vs Debug Mode"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Reference-Tests-(reftest)","page":"Developer Guide","text":"BoltzTraP.jl includes 75 reference tests that validate numerical equivalence with Python BoltzTraP2. These tests compare Julia output against pre-computed Python reference data.\n\nFor complete documentation on running and extending reference tests, see the dedicated Reference Tests page.\n\nQuick start:\n\n# Generate reference data (requires Python BoltzTraP2)\ncd reftest\npip install boltztrap2\npython generate_1_sphere.py\npython generate_2_interpolation_si.py\n# ... (see reftest.md for full list)\n\n# Run tests\ncd ..\njulia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'","title":"Reference Tests (reftest)"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Functional-Tests-(ftest)","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Functional tests are located in the ftest/ directory. Unlike unit tests in test/, these are standalone scripts for manual verification and debugging.","title":"Functional Tests (ftest)"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Available-Tests","page":"Developer Guide","text":"File Purpose\ntest_cli_all_options.jl Comprehensive test of all CLI options\ntest_cli_args.jl Basic argument propagation test\ntest_format_detection.jl DFT format auto-detection test","title":"Available Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Running-Functional-Tests","page":"Developer Guide","text":"# Run from project root\ncd BoltzTraP.jl\n\n# Test all CLI options (requires VASP test data)\njulia --project=. ftest/test_cli_all_options.jl\n\n# With custom data directory\njulia --project=. ftest/test_cli_all_options.jl /path/to/vasp/dir\n\n# Test format detection\njulia --project=. ftest/test_format_detection.jl\n\n# With debug output\nJULIA_DEBUG=BoltzTraP julia --project=. ftest/test_cli_args.jl","title":"Running Functional Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Test-Data","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Functional tests expect VASP data in ../BoltzTraP2-public/data/Si.vasp/ by default. You can specify a different directory as a command-line argument.\n\nRequired files:\n\nvasp_directory/\n└── vasprun.xml","title":"Test Data"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Writing-New-Functional-Tests","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Template for a new functional test:\n\n# ftest/test_new_feature.jl\nusing Logging\n\n# Enable debug logging\nENV[\"JULIA_DEBUG\"] = \"BoltzTraP\"\nglobal_logger(ConsoleLogger(stderr, Logging.Debug))\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\nfunction main()\n    println(\"=\" ^ 60)\n    println(\"Functional Test: New Feature\")\n    println(\"=\" ^ 60)\n\n    # Test 1\n    println(\"-\" ^ 60)\n    println(\"Test 1: Description\")\n    println(\"-\" ^ 60)\n    @info \"Running test 1\"\n\n    # ... test code ...\n\n    @assert condition \"Error message\"\n    println(\"  ✓ PASS\")\n\n    println(\"=\" ^ 60)\n    println(\"All tests passed!\")\n    println(\"=\" ^ 60)\nend\n\nmain()","title":"Writing New Functional Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#DFT-Format-Auto-Detection","page":"Developer Guide","text":"","title":"DFT Format Auto-Detection"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#How-It-Works","page":"Developer Guide","text":"The CLI uses load_dft() to automatically detect the DFT format:\n\nTry each registered loader in reverse order (VASP first, then QE)\nEach loader has a detector function that checks for format-specific files\nFirst successful detection wins","title":"How It Works"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Detector-Functions","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Format Detection\nVASP vasprun.xml exists in directory\nQE *.save/data-file-schema.xml or *.save/data-file.xml exists\nWien2k case.struct + case.energy (or variants) exist\nGENE case.structure + case.energy exist\nABINIT *_GSR.nc NetCDF file exists (requires NCDatasets.jl)","title":"Detector Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Forcing-a-Format","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Use --format to skip auto-detection:\n\nboltztrap interpolate ./data -f vasp  # Force VASP\nboltztrap interpolate ./data -f qe    # Force QE\nboltztrap interpolate ./data -f auto  # Auto-detect (default)","title":"Forcing a Format"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Adding-a-New-Format","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Create loader in src/io/newformat.jl\nRegister in src/io/loader.jl:\n\nfunction _detect_newformat(directory::String)\n    # Return true if format detected\n    isfile(joinpath(directory, \"newformat.out\"))\nend\n\n# In _init_loaders():\nregister_loader(\"NewFormat\", _detect_newformat, load_newformat)","title":"Adding a New Format"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Code-Structure","page":"Developer Guide","text":"src/\n├── cli.jl              # CLI commands (Comonicon)\n├── workflow.jl         # High-level workflows with @debug logging\n├── io/\n│   ├── loader.jl       # Auto-detection logic\n│   ├── vasp.jl         # VASP loader\n│   ├── qe.jl           # QE loader\n│   ├── wien2k.jl       # Wien2k loader\n│   ├── gene.jl         # GENE/Generic loader\n│   ├── abinit.jl       # ABINIT loader (extension)\n│   └── serialization.jl # JLD2/BT2 serialization\n└── ...\n\nftest/                  # Functional tests (manual)\ntest/                   # Unit tests (automated)","title":"Code Structure"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Troubleshooting","page":"Developer Guide","text":"","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#\"No-compatible-DFT-format-found\"","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Check directory path is correct\nRun with --debug to see detection attempts\nTry explicit format: --format vasp\nVerify required files exist (e.g., vasprun.xml)","title":"\"No compatible DFT format found\""},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Debug-Output-Not-Showing","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Ensure --debug is passed to the command\nOr set ENV[\"JULIA_DEBUG\"] = \"BoltzTraP\" before using BoltzTraP\nCheck stderr (debug goes to stderr, not stdout)","title":"Debug Output Not Showing"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Functional-Test-Failures","page":"Developer Guide","text":"Check test data path exists\nRun with debug logging enabled\nCheck assertion error messages for details","title":"Functional Test Failures"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Migration-Guide","page":"Developer Guide","text":"","title":"Migration Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#v0.1-v0.3:-Collinear-Magnetic-Support","page":"Developer Guide","text":"v0.3 introduces full support for spin-polarized (collinear magnetic) materials.\n\nKey changes:\n\nFeature v0.1 v0.3\nMaterial types Non-magnetic only Non-magnetic + Collinear\nDFTData type DFTData{1} only DFTData{N} where N∈{1,2}\nBand structure ebands[nbands, nkpts] ebands[nbands, nkpts, nspin]\ndosweight Always 2.0 2.0 (non-mag) or 1.0 (collinear)\nspintype metadata Always \"Unpolarized\" \"Unpolarized\" or \"Collinear\"\n\nBackward compatibility:\n\nv0.1 files (.jld2, .bt2) are fully compatible with v0.3\nv0.1 code continues to work unchanged for non-magnetic materials\nSpin-polarized data requires nspin=2 in the band structure\n\nCode changes for collinear support:\n\n# v0.1 (non-magnetic only)\ndata = load_vasp(\"./Si.vasp\")          # DFTData{1}\ninterp = run_interpolate(data)\ntransport = run_integrate(interp)\n\n# v0.3 (same API, automatic spin detection)\ndata = load_vasp(\"./Fe.vasp\")          # DFTData{2} if spin-polarized\ninterp = run_interpolate(data)         # Automatic collinear handling\ntransport = run_integrate(interp)\n\n# Check spin type\nprintln(is_magnetic(data))              # true for DFTData{2}\nprintln(transport.metadata[\"spintype\"]) # \"Collinear\"\n\nNew functions in v0.3:\n\nFunction Description\nis_magnetic(data) Check if data is spin-polarized\nnspin(data) Get number of spin channels (1 or 2)\n\nInternal changes:\n\nDFTData{N} parametric type with N = number of spin channels\ndetermine_compatibility considers magnetic moments in symmetry\nTransport integration handles spin channels separately then combines","title":"v0.1 → v0.3: Collinear Magnetic Support"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Architecture","page":"Architecture","text":"This page describes BoltzTraP.jl's transport API design, in particular the dispatch axes that allow swapping the BZ sampling and band interpolation strategies independently.","title":"Architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Method-dispatched-transport-API","page":"Architecture","text":"The primary entry point for transport coefficient computation is solve_transport, which dispatches on three independent axes: sampling::AbstractBZSampling, interp::AbstractInterpolator, and sys::TransportSystem.\n\nAxis Abstract type Concrete subtype(s) Role\nsampling AbstractBZSampling UniformMesh How the BZ is sampled\ninterp AbstractInterpolator FourierInterpolator How band energies and velocities are evaluated\nsys TransportSystem (concrete struct) Material data carrier\n\nA new sampling or interpolator strategy can be added by introducing a new concrete subtype and a new solve_transport method dispatched on that combination — no changes to existing types or to the entry point.","title":"Method-dispatched transport API"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#BZ-sampling","page":"Architecture","text":"","title":"BZ sampling"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Material-data-carrier","page":"Architecture","text":"TransportSystem collects material-dependent data that was previously scattered across InterpolationResult.metadata and BandData{ST}:\n\nThe TransportSystem(::InterpolationResult) convenience constructor extracts these fields from existing interpolation output, so callers that already have an InterpolationResult need not assemble the struct manually.","title":"Material data carrier"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Backward-compatibility","page":"Architecture","text":"The legacy entry points\n\nrun_integrate(interp::InterpolationResult; kwargs...)\nrun_integrate(interp::InterpolationResult, mur::AbstractVector; kwargs...)\n\nare preserved as thin wrappers. They build a TransportSystem and FourierInterpolator from the InterpolationResult and delegate to solve_transport(UniformMesh(), ...). The wrapper restores the original metadata[\"source\"] value after the delegate returns. Output is bit-equal to the pre-refactor implementation under default arguments.","title":"Backward compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Provenance-metadata","page":"Architecture","text":"TransportResult.metadata records which sampling and interpolator types produced the output:\n\nKey Example value Source\n\"sampling\" \"UniformMesh\" type name of the sampling argument\n\"interpolator\" \"Fourier\" type name of the interpolator argument with the Interpolator suffix stripped\n\nThese keys are stamped by solve_transport and survive JLD2 round-trip through save_integrate / load_integrate.","title":"Provenance metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"architecture/#BoltzTraP.solve_transport","page":"Architecture","text":"solve_transport(sampling, interp, sys; kwargs...) -> TransportResult\n\nMethod-dispatched entry point for transport calculations on the AbstractBZSampling × AbstractInterpolator × TransportSystem axes.\n\nMethod overloads:\n\n::Any fallback: throws ArgumentError naming the offending argument type\n::AbstractBZSampling, ::AbstractInterpolator, ::TransportSystem: throws ErrorException for a sampling subtype that has no concrete method\nconcrete pair (e.g. UniformMesh + FourierInterpolator): actual computation\n\nThe concrete UniformMesh + FourierInterpolator method below mirrors the existing run_integrate(::InterpolationResult) flow so its output is bit-equal under default kwargs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.solve_transport"},{"category":"type","location":"architecture/#BoltzTraP.AbstractBZSampling","page":"Architecture","text":"AbstractBZSampling\n\nAbstract type for Brillouin-zone sampling strategies used by transport calculations. Concrete subtypes (e.g. UniformMesh) carry the parameters that determine how solve_transport integrates over the BZ.\n\nThis is the dispatch axis for BZ sampling strategies; alternative methods (adaptive grids, AutoBZ-style integration, wavelet meshes) can be added later as additional concrete subtypes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.AbstractBZSampling"},{"category":"type","location":"architecture/#BoltzTraP.UniformMesh","page":"Architecture","text":"UniformMesh(nk=nothing)\nUniformMesh((nk1, nk2, nk3))\n\nUniform Monkhorst-Pack-style mesh. The nk field selects the mesh density:\n\nnk === nothing: the interpolator decides the mesh size (matches the legacy run_integrate behavior, which derives the mesh from the interpolator's k-point density).\nnk::Tuple{Int,Int,Int}: explicit user-specified mesh dimensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.UniformMesh"},{"category":"type","location":"architecture/#BoltzTraP.TransportSystem","page":"Architecture","text":"TransportSystem(lattice, fermi, nelect, dosweight, spintype)\nTransportSystem(interp::InterpolationResult)\n\nSystem-level constants needed for transport calculations: lattice, Fermi energy, electron count, DOS weight, and spin type. These quantities are conventionally spread across InterpolationResult.metadata and the underlying band data; this struct unifies them as a single, dispatched data carrier for the new solve_transport API.\n\nFields:\n\nlattice::SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9}: 3×3 lattice vectors (columns) in Bohr\nfermi::Float64: Fermi energy in Hartree\nnelect::Float64: number of electrons per unit cell\ndosweight::Float64: DOS weight (2.0 = non-magnetic, 1.0 = spin-polarized)\nspintype::SpinType: Unpolarized() / Collinear() / NonCollinear()\n\nThe TransportSystem(::InterpolationResult) constructor is defined in io/serialization.jl (after InterpolationResult itself is defined).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.TransportSystem"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#Functional-Tests","page":"Functional Tests","text":"BoltzTraP.jl provides functional tests (ftest) for verifying CLI commands and end-to-end workflows. These are standalone scripts for manual execution, useful for:\n\nInstallation verification: Confirm BoltzTraP.jl works correctly after installation\nTroubleshooting: Diagnose issues with CLI commands or format detection\nIntegration testing: Verify DFTK integration works in your environment","title":"Functional Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#Overview","page":"Functional Tests","text":"Aspect Description\nLocation ftest/ directory\nExecution Manual (julia --project ftest/script.jl)\nIncluded in Pkg.test() No\nRequirements BoltzTraP2-public test data (for most scripts)\n\nnote: ftest vs test\ntest/: Automated unit tests, run via Pkg.test()\nftest/: Manual functional tests, run individually","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#Available-Scripts","page":"Functional Tests","text":"Script Purpose Requirements\ntest_cli_all_options.jl Test all CLI options for interpolate/integrate VASP data\ntest_cli_args.jl Test CLI argument propagation VASP data\ntest_cli_bt2.jl Test CLI with Python-generated .bt2 files .bt2 file\ntest_format_detection.jl Test DFT format auto-detection Multiple formats\ntest_dftk_e2e.jl End-to-end DFTK integration DFTK.jl","title":"Available Scripts"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#Quick-Start","page":"Functional Tests","text":"","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#Verify-Installation","page":"Functional Tests","text":"After installing BoltzTraP.jl, run the format detection test:\n\ncd BoltzTraP.jl\njulia --project ftest/test_format_detection.jl\n\nExpected output:\n\n============================================================\nFunctional Test: DFT Format Auto-Detection\n============================================================\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\nTest 1: Detect VASP format\n------------------------------------------------------------\n  Directory: ../BoltzTraP2-public/data/Si.vasp\n  Detected: VASP\n  ✓ PASS\n...","title":"Verify Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#Test-CLI-Commands","page":"Functional Tests","text":"# Test all CLI options (comprehensive)\njulia --project ftest/test_cli_all_options.jl\n\n# Test with custom data directory\njulia --project ftest/test_cli_all_options.jl /path/to/vasp/data\n\n# Basic argument test\njulia --project ftest/test_cli_args.jl","title":"Test CLI Commands"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#Test-DFTK-Integration","page":"Functional Tests","text":"# Requires DFTK.jl (takes ~1 minute for SCF)\njulia --project ftest/test_dftk_e2e.jl\n\nThis runs a complete workflow: DFTK SCF → load_dftk → run_interpolate → run_integrate.","title":"Test DFTK Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#Test-.bt2-File-Support","page":"Functional Tests","text":"# Test Python BoltzTraP2 .bt2 file compatibility\njulia --project ftest/test_cli_bt2.jl","title":"Test .bt2 File Support"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#Debug-Output","page":"Functional Tests","text":"Enable debug logging for detailed output:\n\nJULIA_DEBUG=BoltzTraP julia --project ftest/test_cli_args.jl\n\nThis shows:\n\nCLI argument values received\nDFT format detection process\nFile loading details\nWorkflow function parameters","title":"Debug Output"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#Test-Data","page":"Functional Tests","text":"Most functional tests expect BoltzTraP2 test data at ../BoltzTraP2-public/data/:\n\nBoltzTraP2-public/\n└── data/\n    ├── Si.vasp/\n    │   └── vasprun.xml\n    ├── Si.ESPRESSO/\n    │   └── out/\n    └── ...\n\nYou can specify a different directory as a command-line argument:\n\njulia --project ftest/test_cli_all_options.jl /my/custom/vasp/dir","title":"Test Data"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#Troubleshooting","page":"Functional Tests","text":"","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#\"No-compatible-DFT-format-found\"","page":"Functional Tests","text":"Run format detection test with debug output:\nJULIA_DEBUG=BoltzTraP julia --project ftest/test_format_detection.jl\nCheck if required files exist:\nVASP: vasprun.xml\nQE: *.save/data-file-schema.xml\nWien2k: *.struct, *.energy, *.scf\nTry explicit format:\ndata = load_vasp(\"/path/to/dir\")  # Instead of load_dft","title":"\"No compatible DFT format found\""},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#DFTK-Test-Fails","page":"Functional Tests","text":"Ensure DFTK is installed:\nusing Pkg; Pkg.add(\"DFTK\")\nLoad DFTK before BoltzTraP:\nusing DFTK\nusing BoltzTraP\nCheck DFTK version compatibility","title":"DFTK Test Fails"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#Test-Script-Not-Found","page":"Functional Tests","text":"Ensure you're in the BoltzTraP.jl directory:\n\ncd /path/to/BoltzTraP.jl\njulia --project ftest/test_format_detection.jl","title":"Test Script Not Found"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#Writing-Custom-Functional-Tests","page":"Functional Tests","text":"Template for a new functional test:\n\n# ftest/test_my_feature.jl\nusing Logging\n\n# Enable debug logging (optional)\nENV[\"JULIA_DEBUG\"] = \"BoltzTraP\"\nglobal_logger(ConsoleLogger(stderr, Logging.Debug))\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\nfunction main()\n    println(\"=\" ^ 60)\n    println(\"Functional Test: My Feature\")\n    println(\"=\" ^ 60)\n    println()\n\n    # Test 1\n    println(\"-\" ^ 60)\n    println(\"Test 1: Description\")\n    println(\"-\" ^ 60)\n\n    result = some_function()\n    @assert condition \"Error message\"\n    println(\"  ✓ PASS\")\n    println()\n\n    # Summary\n    println(\"=\" ^ 60)\n    println(\"All tests passed!\")\n    println(\"=\" ^ 60)\nend\n\nmain()","title":"Writing Custom Functional Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"ftest/#See-Also","page":"Functional Tests","text":"Validation - Visual comparison with Python BoltzTraP2\nReference Tests - Numerical equivalence tests","title":"See Also"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#CLI-Workflow","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"This page describes the CLI workflow - using BoltzTraP.jl from the command line.\n\ninfo: CLI vs API Workflow\nCLI workflow: Run calculations via boltztrap shell command\nAPI workflow: Call Julia functions (run_interpolate, run_integrate) directlyFor API workflow, see API Workflow.","title":"CLI Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#Prerequisites","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"BoltzTraP.jl installed (see Installation)\nCLI set up (see CLI Setup) — requires additional step after using BoltzTraP\nDFT calculation results (VASP, Quantum ESPRESSO, or other supported formats)\n\n","title":"Prerequisites"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#Workflow-Overview","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"DFT Output (vasprun.xml)\n         │\n         ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  boltztrap interpolate ./Si.vasp   │\n└─────────────────────────────────────┘\n         │\n         ▼\n   interpolation.jld2\n         │\n         ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  boltztrap integrate interp.jld2   │\n│               -t 300               │\n└─────────────────────────────────────┘\n         │\n         ▼\n   transport.jld2 (σ, S, κ)\n\n","title":"Workflow Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#Commands","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"","title":"Commands"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#interpolate","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"tip: API Workflow\nFor programmatic access, see run_interpolate in the API Workflow page.\n\n","title":"interpolate"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#integrate","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"tip: API Workflow\nFor programmatic access, see run_integrate in the API Workflow page.\n\n","title":"integrate"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#describe","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"Inspect interpolation or transport result files. See Output Formats for details.\n\nboltztrap describe interpolation.jld2\nboltztrap describe transport.jld2\n\n","title":"describe"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#plot","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"","title":"plot"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#plotbands","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"","title":"plotbands"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#Complete-Example","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"# 1. Prepare VASP calculation directory\nls ./Si.vasp/\n# vasprun.xml  POSCAR\n\n# 2. Run interpolation\nboltztrap interpolate ./Si.vasp -k 5000 -v\n# Output: Si.vasp_interp.jld2\n\n# 3. Compute transport at multiple temperatures\n# Note: -t accepts a scalar (e.g., 300) or Julia range \"start:step:stop\"\nboltztrap integrate Si.vasp_interp.jld2 -t \"100:100:800\" -v\n# Output: Si.vasp_interp_transport.jld2\n\n# 4. (Optional) Load results in Julia for analysis\njulia -e '\nusing JLD2, BoltzTraP\ntransport = load_integrate(\"Si.vasp_interp_transport.jld2\")\nprintln(\"Temperatures: \", transport.temperatures)\nprintln(\"Seebeck at 300K: \", transport.seebeck[1,1,3,:])\n'\n\n","title":"Complete Example"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#Help","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"# General help\nboltztrap --help\n\n# Command-specific help\nboltztrap interpolate --help\nboltztrap integrate --help\nboltztrap describe --help\nboltztrap plot --help\nboltztrap plotbands --help\n\n","title":"Help"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#Next-Steps","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"Input Formats - DFT input formats and loaders\nOutput Formats - Result file formats\nAPI Workflow - Using Julia API for custom analysis","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"function","location":"cli/#BoltzTraP.interpolate","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"interpolate(directory; format=\"auto\", output=\"\", kpoints=0, multiplier=0, emin=-Inf, emax=Inf, absolute=false, verbose=false, debug=false)\n\nInterpolate band structure from DFT data.\n\nRead DFT output and compute Fourier interpolation coefficients for band energies. Support VASP, Quantum ESPRESSO, and other formats.\n\nArguments\n\ndirectory: Directory containing DFT output files\n\nOptions\n\n-f, --format <fmt>: DFT format (auto, vasp, qe). Default: auto-detect\n-o, --output <file>: Output file path (default: based on input directory)\n-k, --kpoints <n>: Target number of k-points/equivalences (default: 5000)\n-m, --multiplier <n>: Multiplier for k-points (alternative to –kpoints)\n--emin <e>: Minimum energy relative to Fermi in Ha (default: -Inf)\n--emax <e>: Maximum energy relative to Fermi in Ha (default: +Inf)\n\nFlags\n\n--absolute: Interpret emin/emax as absolute energies\n-v, --verbose: Print progress information\n--debug: Enable debug logging (detailed internal info)\n\nExamples\n\nboltztrap interpolate ./Si.vasp\nboltztrap interpolate ./Si.vasp -f vasp -o si_interp.jld2 -k 10000\nboltztrap interpolate ./Si.qe -f qe --emin -0.5 --emax 0.5 -v\nboltztrap interpolate ./calculation --debug  # with debug output\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.interpolate"},{"category":"function","location":"cli/#BoltzTraP.integrate","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"integrate(input; temperature=\"300\", output=\"\", bins=0, scissor=NaN, verbose=false, debug=false)\n\nCompute transport coefficients from interpolated band structure.\n\nRead interpolation result and compute electrical conductivity, Seebeck coefficient, and thermal conductivity.\n\nArguments\n\ninput: Path to interpolation result file (.jld2 or .bt2)\n\nOptions\n\n-t, --temperature <T>: Temperature(s) in K. Formats:\nSingle value: \"300\" → [300.0]\nRange: \"100:500:50\" (start:stop:step) → [100.0, 150.0, 200.0, ..., 500.0]\nList: \"100,200,300\" → [100.0, 200.0, 300.0]\n-o, --output <file>: Output file path (default: based on input)\n-b, --bins <n>: Number of DOS histogram bins (default: auto)\n-s, --scissor <eV>: Target band gap in eV for scissor correction. Shifts conduction bands to achieve the specified gap. Only for semiconductors.\n\nFlags\n\n-v, --verbose: Print progress information\n--debug: Enable debug logging (detailed internal info)\n\nExamples\n\nboltztrap integrate si_interp.jld2 -t 300\nboltztrap integrate si_interp.jld2 -t \"100:500:50\" -o results.jld2\nboltztrap integrate si_interp.jld2 -t 300 --scissor 1.1\nboltztrap integrate si_interp.jld2 -t \"100,200,300,400,500\" -v --debug\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.integrate"},{"category":"function","location":"cli/#BoltzTraP.plot","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"plot(file; quantity=\"sigma\", component=\"xx\", abscissa=\"mu\", temperature=300.0, mu=NaN, output=\"\")\n\nPlot transport coefficients from integration result.\n\nPlot transport properties (σ, S, κ, etc.) as a function of chemical potential or temperature.\n\nArguments\n\nfile: Path to transport result file (.jld2)\n\nOptions\n\n-q, --quantity <name>: Quantity to plot (sigma, seebeck, kappa, dos, n). Default: sigma\n-c, --component <ij>: Tensor component (xx, yy, zz, xy, xz, yz). Default: xx\n-a, --abscissa <var>: X-axis variable (mu or T). Default: mu\n-t, --temperature <T>: Temperature in K for mu plot. Default: 300\n-m, --mu <μ>: Chemical potential in Ha for T plot. Default: Fermi energy\n-o, --output <file>: Output file (PNG/PDF). Default: display\n\nExamples\n\nboltztrap plot transport.jld2 -q sigma -c xx\nboltztrap plot transport.jld2 -q seebeck -a T -m 0.0\nboltztrap plot transport.jld2 -q sigma -t 300 -o sigma_300K.png\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.plot"},{"category":"function","location":"cli/#BoltzTraP.plotbands","page":"CLI Workflow","text":"plotbands(file; npoints=100, emin=-5.0, emax=5.0, no_fermi=false, kpath=\"\", output=\"\")\n\nPlot band structure along high-symmetry k-path.\n\nGenerate band structure plot from interpolation result using Brillouin.jl to determine the k-path for the crystal's space group. For .bt2 files without spacegroup metadata, use –kpath to specify manually.\n\nArguments\n\nfile: Path to interpolation result file (.jld2 or .bt2)\n\nOptions\n\n-n, --npoints <n>: Number of k-points per path segment. Default: 100\n--emin <e>: Minimum energy relative to Fermi [eV]. Default: -5.0\n--emax <e>: Maximum energy relative to Fermi [eV]. Default: 5.0\n--kpath <spec>: Manual k-path specification (required for .bt2 without spacegroup)\n-o, --output <file>: Output file (PNG/PDF). Default: display\n\nFlags\n\n--no-fermi: Hide Fermi level line\n\nK-path format\n\n--kpath \"label:x,y,z;...|path-path-...\"\n\nExample for FCC (Si): --kpath \"G:0,0,0;X:0.5,0,0.5;L:0.5,0.5,0.5;W:0.5,0.25,0.75|G-X-W-L-G\"\n\nExamples\n\nboltztrap plotbands si_interp.jld2\nboltztrap plotbands si_interp.jld2 --emin -10 --emax 10 -o bands.png\nboltztrap plotbands si_interp.jld2 -n 200 --no-fermi\nboltztrap plotbands si_interp.bt2 --kpath \"G:0,0,0;X:0.5,0,0.5|G-X-G\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.plotbands"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Conventions","page":"Conventions","text":"BoltzTraP.jl follows consistent conventions for arrays and physical units.\n\n","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Array-Conventions","page":"Conventions","text":"","title":"Array Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#K-points","page":"Conventions","text":"K-points are stored as 3×nk matrices (column vectors):\n\nkpoints = [\n    kx₁ kx₂ kx₃ ...\n    ky₁ ky₂ ky₃ ...\n    kz₁ kz₂ kz₃ ...\n]  # 3×nk","title":"K-points"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Band-Energies","page":"Conventions","text":"Band energies are stored as nbands×nk×nspin arrays:\n\nebands = zeros(nbands, nk, nspin)\nebands[iband, ik, ispin] = energy","title":"Band Energies"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Transport-Tensors","page":"Conventions","text":"Transport tensors (σ, S, κ) are stored as 3×3×nT×nμ arrays:\n\nsigma[i, j, iT, imu]  # σ_ij at temperature iT and chemical potential imu\n\nTo get the trace:\n\ntrace_sigma = sigma[1,1,:,:] + sigma[2,2,:,:] + sigma[3,3,:,:]\n\nTo get the average:\n\navg_sigma = (sigma[1,1,:,:] + sigma[2,2,:,:] + sigma[3,3,:,:]) / 3\n\n","title":"Transport Tensors"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Unit-System","page":"Conventions","text":"BoltzTraP.jl follows Python BoltzTraP2 conventions for compatibility.","title":"Unit System"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Summary-by-Module","page":"Conventions","text":"Module Energy Length Notes\nI/O (VASP) eV Å Native VASP format\nInterpolation - - Unit-agnostic\nTransport Hartree Bohr Internal atomic units\nCLI (emin/emax) Hartree - BoltzTraP2 compatible\nOutput Preserves input Preserves input ","title":"Summary by Module"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Interpolation:-Unit-Agnostic","page":"Conventions","text":"The interpolation module performs pure mathematical operations (Fourier fitting, phase factors, band reconstruction) independent of physical units.\n\n# If input is in eV (from VASP), output is in eV\nresult = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\")","title":"Interpolation: Unit-Agnostic"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Transport:-Atomic-Units","page":"Conventions","text":"The transport module uses atomic units internally (ℏ = e = mₑ = kB = 1).\n\nOutput is converted to SI units:\n\nσ: S/m (electrical conductivity)\nS: V/K (Seebeck coefficient)\nκ: W/(m·K) (thermal conductivity)","title":"Transport: Atomic Units"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#CLI-Parameters","page":"Conventions","text":"For Python BoltzTraP2 compatibility, --emin and --emax are in Hartree relative to Fermi:\n\n# Energy window: Fermi ± 0.5 Ha (≈ ±13.6 eV)\nboltztrap interpolate ./Si.vasp --emin -0.5 --emax 0.5","title":"CLI Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Conversion-Reference","page":"Conventions","text":"Quantity Atomic → SI\nEnergy 1 Hartree (Ha) = 27.211 eV\nLength 1 Bohr = 0.529 Å\nTemperature 1 Ha/kB = 315,775 Kelvin (K)\n\n","title":"Conversion Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Type-Summary","page":"Conventions","text":"Type Description\nInterpolationResult Fourier coefficients and metadata\nTransportResult Transport tensors and DOS\nDFTData Abstract type for DFT input data\nNonMagneticData Non-magnetic (unpolarized) DFT data\nSpinPolarizedData Spin-polarized DFT data (defined, full support in v0.3)\n\n","title":"Type Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#DFT-Data-Types","page":"Conventions","text":"","title":"DFT Data Types"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Usage","page":"Conventions","text":"using BoltzTraP\n\n# Load DFT data (returns NonMagneticData for unpolarized calculations)\ndata = load_vasp(\"./Si.vasp\")\n\n# Check spin type\nnspin(data)        # Returns 1 for non-magnetic\nis_magnetic(data)  # Returns false for non-magnetic\n\n# Type dispatch\nfunction process(data::NonMagneticData)\n    # Handle non-magnetic case\nend\n\nnote: Magnetic Material Support (v0.3+)\nSpinPolarizedData provides full support for collinear magnetic calculations. Non-collinear magnetic support is planned for v0.4.","title":"Usage"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BoltzTraP.DFTData","page":"Conventions","text":"DFTData{NSpin}\n\nContainer for DFT calculation results with spin parameter.\n\nType Parameter\n\nNSpin::Int: Number of spin channels\n1: Non-spin-polarized (non-magnetic)\n2: Spin-polarized (collinear magnetic, ISPIN=2)\n\nFields\n\nlattice::Matrix{Float64}: Lattice vectors (3×3) in Bohr, columns are vectors\npositions::Matrix{Float64}: Atomic positions (3×natom) in fractional coordinates\nspecies::Vector{String}: Element symbols\nkpoints::Matrix{Float64}: K-points (3×nkpts) in fractional coordinates\nweights::Vector{Float64}: K-point weights (nkpts,)\nebands::Array{Float64,3}: Band energies (nbands, nkpts, nspin) in Hartree\noccupations::Array{Float64,3}: Occupations (nbands, nkpts, nspin)\nfermi::Float64: Fermi energy in Hartree\nnelect::Float64: Number of electrons\nmagmom::Union{Nothing,Vector{Float64},Matrix{Float64}}: Magnetic moments (optional)\n\nNotes\n\nNon-collinear (spinor) calculations are not supported in v0.1\nWhen NSpin=2, bands from both spin channels are stored separately\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.DFTData"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BoltzTraP.NonMagneticData","page":"Conventions","text":"DFTData{NSpin}\n\nContainer for DFT calculation results with spin parameter.\n\nType Parameter\n\nNSpin::Int: Number of spin channels\n1: Non-spin-polarized (non-magnetic)\n2: Spin-polarized (collinear magnetic, ISPIN=2)\n\nFields\n\nlattice::Matrix{Float64}: Lattice vectors (3×3) in Bohr, columns are vectors\npositions::Matrix{Float64}: Atomic positions (3×natom) in fractional coordinates\nspecies::Vector{String}: Element symbols\nkpoints::Matrix{Float64}: K-points (3×nkpts) in fractional coordinates\nweights::Vector{Float64}: K-point weights (nkpts,)\nebands::Array{Float64,3}: Band energies (nbands, nkpts, nspin) in Hartree\noccupations::Array{Float64,3}: Occupations (nbands, nkpts, nspin)\nfermi::Float64: Fermi energy in Hartree\nnelect::Float64: Number of electrons\nmagmom::Union{Nothing,Vector{Float64},Matrix{Float64}}: Magnetic moments (optional)\n\nNotes\n\nNon-collinear (spinor) calculations are not supported in v0.1\nWhen NSpin=2, bands from both spin channels are stored separately\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.NonMagneticData"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BoltzTraP.SpinPolarizedData","page":"Conventions","text":"DFTData{NSpin}\n\nContainer for DFT calculation results with spin parameter.\n\nType Parameter\n\nNSpin::Int: Number of spin channels\n1: Non-spin-polarized (non-magnetic)\n2: Spin-polarized (collinear magnetic, ISPIN=2)\n\nFields\n\nlattice::Matrix{Float64}: Lattice vectors (3×3) in Bohr, columns are vectors\npositions::Matrix{Float64}: Atomic positions (3×natom) in fractional coordinates\nspecies::Vector{String}: Element symbols\nkpoints::Matrix{Float64}: K-points (3×nkpts) in fractional coordinates\nweights::Vector{Float64}: K-point weights (nkpts,)\nebands::Array{Float64,3}: Band energies (nbands, nkpts, nspin) in Hartree\noccupations::Array{Float64,3}: Occupations (nbands, nkpts, nspin)\nfermi::Float64: Fermi energy in Hartree\nnelect::Float64: Number of electrons\nmagmom::Union{Nothing,Vector{Float64},Matrix{Float64}}: Magnetic moments (optional)\n\nNotes\n\nNon-collinear (spinor) calculations are not supported in v0.1\nWhen NSpin=2, bands from both spin channels are stored separately\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.SpinPolarizedData"},{"category":"function","location":"conventions/#BoltzTraP.nspin","page":"Conventions","text":"nspin(data::DFTData{N}) -> Int\n\nReturn the number of spin channels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.nspin"},{"category":"function","location":"conventions/#BoltzTraP.is_magnetic","page":"Conventions","text":"is_magnetic(data::DFTData) -> Bool\n\nReturn true if data is from spin-polarized calculation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.is_magnetic"},{"category":"section","location":"integrate/#Integration","page":"Integration","text":"Compute transport coefficients by Brillouin zone integration.","title":"Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"integrate/#run_integrate","page":"Integration","text":"","title":"run_integrate"},{"category":"section","location":"integrate/#Example","page":"Integration","text":"using BoltzTraP\n\n# Basic usage\ntransport = run_integrate(\"si_interp.jld2\";\n    temperatures = [300.0]\n)\n\n# Temperature range\ntransport = run_integrate(\"si_interp.jld2\";\n    temperatures = collect(100:50:500),  # 100, 150, ..., 500 K\n    bins = 2000,\n    verbose = true\n)\n\n# Save results\nsave_integrate(\"si_transport.jld2\", transport)\n\n","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"integrate/#Output:-save/load","page":"Integration","text":"","title":"Output: save/load"},{"category":"section","location":"integrate/#Example-2","page":"Integration","text":"# Save integration results\nsave_integrate(\"si_transport.jld2\", transport)\n\n# Load for analysis\nloaded = load_integrate(\"si_transport.jld2\")\n\n# Access transport tensors\nsigma = loaded.sigma      # 3×3×nT×nμ conductivity\nseebeck = loaded.seebeck  # 3×3×nT×nμ Seebeck coefficient\nkappa = loaded.kappa      # 3×3×nT×nμ thermal conductivity","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"integrate/#Supported-Formats","page":"Integration","text":"Extension Format Description\n.jld2 JLD2 Julia native (full tensor data)\n.csv CSV Text format (trace averages only)\n\n","title":"Supported Formats"},{"category":"section","location":"integrate/#Analyzing-Results","page":"Integration","text":"","title":"Analyzing Results"},{"category":"section","location":"integrate/#Temperature-Dependence","page":"Integration","text":"# Get Seebeck at chemical potential μ=0 for all temperatures\nidx_mu0 = argmin(abs.(transport.mu_values))\nS_vs_T = transport.seebeck[1, 1, :, idx_mu0] .* 1e6  # V/K → μV/K\n\nusing Plots\nplot(transport.temperatures, S_vs_T,\n    xlabel = \"Temperature (K)\",\n    ylabel = \"S_xx (μV/K)\",\n    title = \"Seebeck coefficient\"\n)","title":"Temperature Dependence"},{"category":"section","location":"integrate/#Chemical-Potential-Dependence","page":"Integration","text":"# Get conductivity at 300K vs chemical potential\nT_idx = findfirst(==(300.0), transport.temperatures)\nsigma_vs_mu = transport.sigma[1, 1, T_idx, :]\n\nplot(transport.mu_values, sigma_vs_mu,\n    xlabel = \"μ (eV)\",\n    ylabel = \"σ_xx (S/m)\",\n    title = \"Conductivity at 300K\"\n)\n\n","title":"Chemical Potential Dependence"},{"category":"section","location":"integrate/#Working-with-TransportResult","page":"Integration","text":"See TransportResult in Output Formats for field details.","title":"Working with TransportResult"},{"category":"section","location":"integrate/#Accessing-Fields","page":"Integration","text":"transport = run_integrate(\"si_interp.jld2\"; temperatures=[300.0, 400.0, 500.0])\n\n# Access temperatures\nprintln(\"Temperatures: \", transport.temperatures)  # [300.0, 400.0, 500.0]\n\n# Seebeck coefficient at 300K (first temperature)\nS_300K = transport.seebeck[:, :, 1, :]  # 3×3×nμ tensor\n\n# Diagonal components\nS_xx = transport.seebeck[1, 1, 1, :]  # S_xx at 300K vs μ\nS_yy = transport.seebeck[2, 2, 1, :]  # S_yy at 300K vs μ\n\n# Find Seebeck at Fermi level (μ = 0)\nidx = argmin(abs.(transport.mu_values))\nS_at_fermi = transport.seebeck[1, 1, 1, idx]\n\n","title":"Accessing Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"integrate/#Next-Steps","page":"Integration","text":"Output Formats - Result file formats and describe command\nConventions - Units and array conventions","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"function","location":"integrate/#BoltzTraP.run_integrate","page":"Integration","text":"run_integrate(interp::InterpolationResult; kwargs...) -> TransportResult\nrun_integrate(interp::InterpolationResult, mur; kwargs...) -> TransportResult\nrun_integrate(input::String; kwargs...) -> TransportResult\nrun_integrate(input::String, mur; kwargs...) -> TransportResult\n\nCompute transport coefficients from interpolation result.\n\nArguments\n\ninterp: InterpolationResult from run_interpolate\ninput: Path to interpolation result file (.jld2) - alternative to interp\nmur: Chemical potential values in Hartree (positional argument, optional). Same name as Python BoltzTraP2 for compatibility.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\ntemperatures=[300.0]: Vector of temperatures in K\noutput=nothing: Output file path (no file saved if nothing)\nbins=0: Number of DOS histogram bins (auto if 0)\nscissor=nothing: Target band gap in eV for scissor correction. If specified, conduction bands are shifted to achieve this gap. Only applicable to semiconductors with a band gap.\nverbose=false: Print progress information\n\nReturns\n\nTransportResult containing σ, S, κ tensors, DOS, and metadata.\n\nExamples\n\n# Direct from InterpolationResult (μ auto-generated)\ninterp = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\")\ntransport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures = [300.0])\n\n# From file\ntransport = run_integrate(\"si_interp.jld2\"; temperatures = [200.0, 300.0, 400.0])\n\n# With explicit μ grid (positional argument, same as Python BoltzTraP2)\nmur = range(-0.5, 0.5, length = 100) .* EV_TO_HA  # eV to Ha\ntransport = run_integrate(interp, mur; temperatures = [300.0])\n\n# With scissor correction (shift conduction bands to 1.1 eV gap)\ntransport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures = [300.0], scissor = 1.1)\n\nSee also: run_interpolate, save_integrate, apply_scissor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.run_integrate"},{"category":"function","location":"integrate/#BoltzTraP.save_integrate","page":"Integration","text":"save_integrate(filename, result::TransportResult)\n\nSave integration results to file.\n\nSupported formats:\n\n.jld2: Julia native (full data)\n.csv: Text format (scalar averages only)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.save_integrate"},{"category":"function","location":"integrate/#BoltzTraP.load_integrate","page":"Integration","text":"load_integrate(filename) -> TransportResult\n\nLoad integration results from file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.load_integrate"},{"category":"section","location":"input_formats/#input-formats","page":"Input Formats","text":"BoltzTraP.jl supports multiple DFT output formats. Each format has a dedicated loader function that returns a DFTData struct.\n\nFormat Input Files Loader\nVASP vasprun.xml load_vasp\nQuantum ESPRESSO data-file-schema.xml load_qe\nWien2k case.struct, case.energy load_wien2k\nGENE/Generic case.structure, case.energy load_gene\nABINIT *_GSR.nc (NetCDF) load_abinit\nDFTK.jl scfres from DFTK.jl load_dftk\n\n","title":"Input Formats"},{"category":"section","location":"input_formats/#Format-Detection","page":"Input Formats","text":"","title":"Format Detection"},{"category":"section","location":"input_formats/#input-vasp","page":"Input Formats","text":"Required files:\n\nFile Required Description\nvasprun.xml Yes Band energies, k-points, lattice, Fermi energy\nPOSCAR No Crystal structure (also in vasprun.xml)\nEIGENVAL No Alternative band energy source\n\nDirectory structure:\n\nSi.vasp/\n├── vasprun.xml    # Main data source\n├── POSCAR         # Optional\n└── EIGENVAL       # Optional\n\n","title":"VASP"},{"category":"section","location":"input_formats/#input-qe","page":"Input Formats","text":"Required files:\n\nFile Required Description\n*.xml or data-file-schema.xml Yes Main XML from pw.x\nbands.dat.gnu Optional Band data from bands.x\n\nSupported calculations:\n\nNon-magnetic (nspin=1)\nCollinear spin-polarized (nspin=2, lsda=true)\n\nNot supported: Non-collinear / spin-orbit calculations\n\nDirectory structure:\n\nSi.ESPRESSO/out/\n└── silicon.xml    # or data-file-schema.xml\n\n","title":"Quantum ESPRESSO"},{"category":"section","location":"input_formats/#input-wien2k","page":"Input Formats","text":"Required files:\n\nFile Required Description\ncase.struct Yes Crystal structure\ncase.energy or case.energyso Yes Band energies\ncase.scf Yes Fermi level\ncase.mommat2 No Momentum matrix elements\n\nEnergy file variants:\n\nFiles Spin Notes\ncase.energy Non-spin-polarized dosweight = 2.0\ncase.energyso Spin-orbit coupling dosweight = 1.0, NSpin = 1\ncase.energyup + case.energydn Spin-polarized dosweight = 1.0, NSpin = 2\ncase.energysoup + case.energysodn SOC + spin dosweight = 1.0, NSpin = 2\n\nSupported lattice types:\n\nCode Lattice Type\nP Primitive\nH Hexagonal\nR Rhombohedral\nF Face-centered\nB Body-centered (I)\nCXY, CXZ, CYZ Base-centered (C, B, A)\n\nDirectory structure:\n\nSi/\n├── Si.struct      # Crystal structure\n├── Si.scf         # SCF output (Fermi level)\n├── Si.energy      # Band energies\n└── Si.mommat2     # Optional momentum matrix\n\n","title":"Wien2k"},{"category":"section","location":"input_formats/#input-gene","page":"Input Formats","text":"Required files:\n\nFile Required Description\ncase.structure Yes Crystal structure (lattice + atomic positions)\ncase.energy Yes Band energies and Fermi level\n\nFile formats:\n\n.structure format:\nLine 1: Title (ignored)\nLines 2-4: Lattice vectors (3x3, Bohr, row-major)\nLine 5: Number of atoms\nLines 6+: Element symbol + Cartesian coordinates (Bohr)\n.energy format:\nLine 1: Title (ignored)\nLine 2: nk nspin efermi(Ry)\nFor each spin channel, for each k-point:\nHeader: kx ky kz nband\nBand lines: energy [vx vy vz] (Rydberg units)\n\nSpin handling:\n\nFor spin-polarized calculations (nspin=2), spin channels are concatenated\nStored as DFTData{1} with doubled band count and dosweight=1.0\n\nDirectory structure:\n\nSi.GENE/\n├── Si.structure   # Crystal structure\n└── Si.energy      # Band energies\n\n","title":"GENE/Generic"},{"category":"section","location":"input_formats/#input-abinit","page":"Input Formats","text":"Required files:\n\nFile Required Description\n*_GSR.nc Yes Ground State Results (NetCDF format)\n\nInstallation:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"NCDatasets\")\n\nNetCDF variables used:\n\nVariable Shape Description\nprimitive_vectors (3, 3) Lattice vectors (Bohr)\nreduced_atom_positions (3, natom) Fractional coordinates\natom_species (natom,) Species index (1-based)\natom_species_names (80, ntypat) Element names\nreduced_coordinates_of_kpoints (3, nkpt) K-points (fractional)\neigenvalues (nband, nkpt, nspin) Band energies (Hartree)\nfermie scalar Fermi energy (Hartree)\nnelect scalar Number of electrons\n\nDirectory structure:\n\nSi.abinit/\n└── outsi_DS1_GSR.nc   # NetCDF Ground State Results\n\n","title":"ABINIT"},{"category":"section","location":"input_formats/#input-dftk","page":"Input Formats","text":"See DFTK.jl Integration for detailed documentation.\n\n","title":"DFTK.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"input_formats/#Test-Data","page":"Input Formats","text":"BoltzTraP.jl includes synthetic test data for all supported formats in test/synthetic_data/:\n\nStructure Format\nMonoclinic ABINIT, GENE, QE, VASP, Wien2k\nTriclinic ABINIT, GENE, QE, VASP, Wien2k\n\nThese files are generated by test/generate_synthetic_data.jl (pure Julia, no external dependencies). For the Python-based generator, see Reference Tests page.\n\nnote: Validation Reference Data\nFor real material data (Si, PbTe, Li, etc.) used in validation against Python BoltzTraP2, see Validation page.\n\n","title":"Test Data"},{"category":"section","location":"input_formats/#Next-Steps","page":"Input Formats","text":"Output Formats - Result file formats and describe command\nAPI Workflow - Using loaders in Julia code\nCLI Workflow - Command-line interface","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"function","location":"input_formats/#BoltzTraP.detected_format","page":"Input Formats","text":"detected_format(directory::String) -> Union{String, Nothing}\n\nCheck which DFT format is detected in the directory without loading.\n\nReturns\n\nFormat name (e.g., \"VASP\", \"QE\") or nothing if no format detected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.detected_format"},{"category":"function","location":"input_formats/#BoltzTraP.load_dft","page":"Input Formats","text":"load_dft(directory::String) -> [`DFTData`](@ref)\n\nAuto-detect DFT format and load band structure data.\n\nTry registered loaders in reverse order (VASP first, then QE, etc.). Throw an error if no compatible format is found.\n\nArguments\n\ndirectory: Path to directory containing DFT output files\n\nReturns\n\nDFTData with band structure data (same format as load_vasp, load_qe).\n\nExample\n\n# Auto-detect format\ndata = load_dft(\"./calculation\")\n\n# Explicit format (still works)\ndata = load_vasp(\"./Si.vasp\")\ndata = load_qe(\"./Si.qe\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.load_dft"},{"category":"function","location":"input_formats/#BoltzTraP.load_vasp","page":"Input Formats","text":"load_vasp(directory) -> [`DFTData`](@ref)\n\nLoad VASP calculation data from directory and convert to atomic units.\n\nReads vasprun.xml for band structure and structure information. Converts from VASP units (eV, Å) to atomic units (Hartree, Bohr).\n\nReturns\n\nDFTData where NSpin is 1 (non-spin-polarized) or 2 (spin-polarized):\nlattice: Lattice vectors (3, 3) in Bohr (columns are vectors)\npositions: Atomic positions (3, natoms) in fractional coordinates\nspecies: Atom species names\nkpoints: K-points (3, nkpts) in fractional coordinates\nweights: K-point weights (nkpts,)\nebands: Band energies (nbands, nkpts, nspin) in Hartree\noccupations: Occupations (nbands, nkpts, nspin)\nfermi: Fermi energy in Hartree\nnelect: Number of electrons\nmagmom: Magnetic moments (nothing for non-spin-polarized)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.load_vasp"},{"category":"function","location":"input_formats/#BoltzTraP.load_qe","page":"Input Formats","text":"load_qe(directory) -> [`DFTData`](@ref)\n\nLoad Quantum ESPRESSO calculation data from directory.\n\nSearches for XML data file (data-file-schema.xml or *.xml in out/ subdirectory). Returns DFTData for non-magnetic or spin-polarized. All data in atomic units (Hartree, Bohr).\n\nArguments\n\ndirectory: Path to QE output directory containing XML file\n\nReturns\n\nDFTData with NSpin ∈ {1, 2}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.load_qe"},{"category":"function","location":"input_formats/#BoltzTraP.load_wien2k","page":"Input Formats","text":"load_wien2k(directory) -> [`DFTData`](@ref)\n\nLoad Wien2k calculation data from directory.\n\nRequired files\n\ncase.struct: Crystal structure\ncase.energy or case.energyso: Band energies\ncase.scf: Fermi level\n\nNotes\n\nSpin-polarized calculations (.energyup/.energydn) are detected but not yet supported\nSOC calculations (.energyso) use dosweight=1.0, resulting in NSpin=2 in DFTData\nAll energies converted from Rydberg to Hartree\nLattice vectors in Bohr\n\nReturns\n\nDFTData where NSpin is determined by dosweight (2.0 → 1, 1.0 → 2)    # Detect files\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.load_wien2k"},{"category":"function","location":"input_formats/#BoltzTraP.load_gene","page":"Input Formats","text":"load_gene(directory::String) -> [`DFTData`](@ref)\n\nLoad GENE/Generic format calculation results.\n\nRequired files\n\ncase.structure: Crystal structure (lattice + atoms)\ncase.energy: Band energies and Fermi level\n\nNotes\n\nFor spin-polarized calculations, bands are concatenated (nspin × nbands)\nAll spin-polarized data is stored as DFTData with dosweight=1.0\nEnergy units: Rydberg → Hartree (×0.5)\nPositions in .structure are Cartesian (Bohr), converted to fractional\n\nReturns\n\nDFTData containing crystal structure and band structure data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.load_gene"},{"category":"function","location":"input_formats/#BoltzTraP.load_abinit","page":"Input Formats","text":"load_abinit(directory::String) -> [`DFTData`](@ref)\n\nLoad DFT data from ABINIT NetCDF output.\n\nArguments\n\ndirectory: Path to directory containing *_GSR.nc file\n\nReturns\n\nDFTData: DFT calculation data\n\nNotes\n\nRequires NCDatasets.jl extension\nInstall with: using Pkg; Pkg.add(\"NCDatasets\")\n\nFile Format\n\nABINIT outputs Ground State Results in NetCDF format:\n\n*_GSR.nc: Contains eigenvalues, k-points, structure, Fermi energy\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.load_abinit"},{"category":"section","location":"wannier/#Wannier.jl-Integration","page":"Wannier.jl Integration","text":"BoltzTraP.jl supports band and velocity interpolation through a Wannier basis via the Wannier.jl package. The Wannier path is loaded as a weak-dependency extension (BoltzTraPWannierExt) and becomes available once using Wannier is in scope alongside using BoltzTraP.\n\nThe extension exposes a WannierInterpolator type that implements the same AbstractInterpolator interface as the default Fourier (Shankland) path. Once constructed, it slots into the existing transport pipeline through solve_transport(::UniformMesh, ::WannierInterpolator, sys).\n\nusing BoltzTraP\nusing Wannier\n\nwi = WannierInterpolator(\"path/to/silicon\")          # prefix entry point\nresult = solve_transport(UniformMesh((8, 8, 8)), wi, sys; temperatures = [300.0])","title":"Wannier.jl Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"wannier/#Construction","page":"Wannier.jl Integration","text":"WannierInterpolator provides two construction entry points. Both produce a value of the same type and feed the same downstream interpolation methods.","title":"Construction"},{"category":"section","location":"wannier/#From-a-Wannier90-file-prefix","page":"Wannier.jl Integration","text":"wi = WannierInterpolator(prefix::String; spintype = Unpolarized())\n\nThe prefix points to a Wannier90 run on disk: <prefix>.win plus the unitary matrices stored in <prefix>.chk (or <prefix>.chk.fmt). The constructor calls Wannier.read_w90_interp internally and then delegates to the in-memory entry point below, centralizing the Ångström → Bohr lattice conversion.\n\nThis is the usual entry point when the Wannierization step has already been performed by an external Wannier90 binary (typical for VASP, QE, Wien2k, or ABINIT pipelines).","title":"From a Wannier90 file prefix"},{"category":"section","location":"wannier/#From-an-in-memory-Wannier.InterpModel","page":"Wannier.jl Integration","text":"wi = WannierInterpolator(imodel::Wannier.InterpModel; spintype = Unpolarized())\n\nThis entry point accepts an InterpModel produced inside the running Julia session, without first writing a .chk binary to disk. Use it when the Wannierization is performed in-process — for example a Wannier.read_w90 followed by disentangle and Wannier.InterpModel, or a DFTK → Wannier.jl pipeline that builds an InterpModel directly from an SCF result.\n\nThe prefix-string constructor is itself a thin wrapper around this method.","title":"From an in-memory Wannier.InterpModel"},{"category":"section","location":"wannier/#Use-with-solve_transport","page":"Wannier.jl Integration","text":"The Wannier path participates in the standard transport pipeline:\n\nresult = solve_transport(mesh::UniformMesh, wi::WannierInterpolator, sys::TransportSystem;\n                         temperatures = ..., ...)","title":"Use with solve_transport"},{"category":"section","location":"wannier/#UniformMesh.nk-is-required","page":"Wannier.jl Integration","text":"The Fourier path can derive a natural integration mesh from the symmetry-equivalent points that accompany the interpolation coefficients. The Wannier path has no such structure — the Wannier.InterpModel carries only the real-space Hamiltonian and the associated R-vectors — so the caller must supply an explicit k-mesh through UniformMesh.nk.\n\nIn practice this means UniformMesh((nk1, nk2, nk3)) with concrete integers; a UniformMesh constructed with nk === nothing is rejected when paired with a WannierInterpolator.\n\nmesh = UniformMesh((8, 8, 8))                            # OK\nresult = solve_transport(mesh, wi, sys; temperatures = [300.0])\n\nmesh_nothing = UniformMesh(nothing)                      # Fourier-only\nsolve_transport(mesh_nothing, wi, sys; ...)              # errors","title":"UniformMesh.nk is required"},{"category":"section","location":"wannier/#Unit-conventions","page":"Wannier.jl Integration","text":"Internally BoltzTraP.jl works in atomic units (Hartree for energy, Bohr for length), to match the rest of the code base. Wannier.jl, in turn, follows the Wannier90 convention of eV for energies and Ångström for lengths.\n\nThe extension performs the conversions inside its interpolate_bands and interpolate_velocities methods, so user-facing arrays returned by these functions are already in BoltzTraP's Hartree/Bohr units:\n\nQuantity Wannier.jl native After extension\nBand energies eV Hartree\nGroup velocities eV·Å Hartree·Bohr\nLattice (constructor) Å Bohr (stored in wi.lattice)\n\nThe numerical conversion factors (HA_TO_EV ≈ 27.2114, BOHR_TO_ANG ≈ 0.5292) are the same constants used by the Fourier path, so the Wannier and Fourier interpolators round-trip with the same definition of \"atomic units\" as the rest of the package.\n\nThe k-point convention also differs by transposition: BoltzTraP.jl takes kpoints as an (nk, 3) matrix, whereas Wannier.jl expects (3, nk). The extension transposes internally so callers continue to use the BoltzTraP shape.","title":"Unit conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"wannier/#Available-methods","page":"Wannier.jl Integration","text":"The methods implemented on top of WannierInterpolator — band interpolation, velocity interpolation, and the solve_transport entry point — share their docstrings with the generic AbstractInterpolator interface, so the same API documentation applies regardless of which interpolator is in use.","title":"Available methods"},{"category":"type","location":"wannier/#BoltzTraP.WannierInterpolator","page":"Wannier.jl Integration","text":"WannierInterpolator(prefix::String; spintype=Unpolarized())\n\nWannier-based interpolator constructed from a Wannier90 file prefix (.chk and related files). Concrete methods for interpolate_bands, interpolate_velocities, and the solve_transport dispatch are provided by BoltzTraPWannierExt, which loads when both Wannier and BoltzTraP are imported.\n\nThe model field is held as Any because Wannier.InterpModel is defined in the optional Wannier.jl package; the stub constructor below errors when using Wannier has not been invoked, ensuring users see a clear message rather than a MethodError.\n\nFields:\n\nmodel::Any: a Wannier.InterpModel instance at runtime\nlattice::SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9}: 3×3 lattice vectors (columns) in Bohr (converted from Wannier's Ångström convention by the extension)\nspintype::ST: Unpolarized() / Collinear() / NonCollinear()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.WannierInterpolator"},{"category":"section","location":"interpolate/#Interpolation","page":"Interpolation","text":"Band structure interpolation using Fourier series fitting.","title":"Interpolation"},{"category":"section","location":"interpolate/#run_interpolate","page":"Interpolation","text":"","title":"run_interpolate"},{"category":"section","location":"interpolate/#Example","page":"Interpolation","text":"using BoltzTraP\n\n# Basic usage\nresult = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\")\n\n# With options\nresult = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\";\n    kpoints = 10000,     # Target equivalence classes\n    emin = -0.5,         # Energy filter (Ha, relative to Fermi)\n    emax = 0.5,\n    verbose = true\n)\n\n# Save to file\nsave_interpolation(\"si_interp.jld2\", result)\n\n","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"interpolate/#Input-Data","page":"Interpolation","text":"run_interpolate accepts:\n\nDirectory path (auto-detects format)\nDFTData from loaders (load_vasp, load_qe, load_wien2k, load_gene, load_abinit, load_dftk)\n\nSee Input Formats for loader documentation and supported file formats.","title":"Input Data"},{"category":"section","location":"interpolate/#Example-2","page":"Interpolation","text":"using BoltzTraP\n\n# Option 1: Directory path (auto-detect format)\nresult = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\")\n\n# Option 2: Load data explicitly\ndata = load_vasp(\"./Si.vasp\")\nresult = run_interpolate(data; source=\"./Si.vasp\")\n\n# Access DFTData fields (all in atomic units: Bohr, Hartree)\ndata.lattice       # 3×3 matrix (Bohr)\ndata.kpoints       # 3×nk matrix (fractional)\ndata.ebands        # nbands×nk×nspin array (Hartree)\ndata.fermi         # Fermi energy (Hartree)\ndata.positions     # 3×natoms matrix (fractional)\ndata.species       # Vector of element symbols\n\n","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"interpolate/#Output:-save/load","page":"Interpolation","text":"","title":"Output: save/load"},{"category":"section","location":"interpolate/#Example-3","page":"Interpolation","text":"# Save interpolation result\nsave_interpolation(\"si_interp.jld2\", result)\n\n# Load for later use\nloaded = load_interpolation(\"si_interp.jld2\")","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"interpolate/#Supported-Formats","page":"Interpolation","text":"Extension Format Description\n.jld2 JLD2 Julia native (HDF5-based, fast)\n.bt2 BT2 Python BoltzTraP2 compatible (JSON + LZMA)\n\n","title":"Supported Formats"},{"category":"section","location":"interpolate/#Working-with-InterpolationResult","page":"Interpolation","text":"See InterpolationResult in Output Formats for field details.","title":"Working with InterpolationResult"},{"category":"section","location":"interpolate/#Accessing-Fields","page":"Interpolation","text":"result = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\")\n\n# Access fields\nprintln(\"Number of bands: \", size(result.coeffs, 1))\nprintln(\"Number of equivalences: \", length(result.equivalences))\nprintln(\"Fermi energy: \", result.metadata[\"fermi_energy\"], \" Ha\")\n\n# Coefficients for band 1\nband1_coeffs = result.coeffs[1, :]\n\n","title":"Accessing Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"interpolate/#Next-Steps","page":"Interpolation","text":"Integration - Compute transport coefficients\nOutput Formats - Result file formats","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"function","location":"interpolate/#BoltzTraP.run_interpolate","page":"Interpolation","text":"run_interpolate(data::DFTData{1}; kwargs...) -> InterpolationResult\n\nRun interpolation workflow on DFTData from loaders.\n\nArguments\n\ndata: DFTData from loaders (load_vasp, load_qe, load_wien2k, load_gene, load_abinit, load_dftk)\n\nSee run_interpolate(::NamedTuple; kwargs...) for keyword arguments.\n\nReturns\n\nInterpolationResult containing coefficients, equivalences, and metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\nrun_interpolate(data::DFTData{2}; kwargs...) -> InterpolationResult\n\nRun interpolation workflow on spin-polarized (collinear) DFTData.\n\nSpin channels are concatenated following Python BoltzTraP2 convention: ebands[:,:,1] and ebands[:,:,2] are vertically concatenated to form (2*nbands, nkpts) before interpolation.\n\nArguments\n\ndata: DFTData{2} from loaders with spin-polarized data\n\nSee run_interpolate(::NamedTuple; kwargs...) for keyword arguments.\n\nReturns\n\nInterpolationResult with metadata[\"spintype\"] = \"Collinear\" and metadata[\"dosweight\"] = 1.0.\n\n\n\n\n\nrun_interpolate(data::NamedTuple; kwargs...) -> InterpolationResult\n\nRun interpolation workflow on pre-loaded band structure data (legacy interface).\n\nArguments\n\ndata: NamedTuple with band structure data (for backward compatibility)\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nsource=\"unknown\": Data source description for metadata\noutput=nothing: Output file path (no file saved if nothing)\nkpoints=5000: Target number of k-points/equivalences\nmultiplier=nothing: Enhancement factor for k-points (alternative to kpoints)\nemin=-Inf: Minimum energy relative to Fermi in Ha (no filter if -Inf)\nemax=+Inf: Maximum energy relative to Fermi in Ha (no filter if +Inf)\nabsolute=false: Interpret emin/emax as absolute energies\nverbose=false: Print progress information\nsymprec=1e-5: Symmetry precision\n\nReturns\n\nInterpolationResult containing coefficients, equivalences, and metadata.\n\nExample\n\n# From VASP\ndata = load_vasp(\"./Si.vasp\")\nresult = run_interpolate(data; source = \"VASP\", kpoints = 5000)\n\n# From QE\ndata = load_qe(\"./Si.qe\")\nresult = run_interpolate(data; source = \"QE\")\n\n# From DFTK (requires DFTK.jl loaded)\ndata = load_dftk(scfres)\nresult = run_interpolate(data; source = \"DFTK\")\n\nSee also: load_vasp, load_qe, load_dftk\n\n\n\n\n\nrun_interpolate(directory::String; kwargs...) -> InterpolationResult\n\nRun the complete interpolation workflow on VASP data.\n\nArguments\n\ndirectory: Path to directory containing vasprun.xml\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\noutput=nothing: Output file path (no file saved if nothing)\nkpoints=5000: Target number of k-points/equivalences\nmultiplier=nothing: Enhancement factor for k-points (alternative to kpoints)\nemin=-Inf: Minimum energy relative to Fermi in Ha (no filter if -Inf)\nemax=+Inf: Maximum energy relative to Fermi in Ha (no filter if +Inf)\nabsolute=false: Interpret emin/emax as absolute energies\nverbose=false: Print progress information\nsymprec=1e-5: Symmetry precision\n\nReturns\n\nInterpolationResult containing coefficients, equivalences, and metadata.\n\nExample\n\nresult = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\"; kpoints = 5000, verbose = true)\n\nSee also: run_integrate, save_interpolation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.run_interpolate"},{"category":"function","location":"interpolate/#BoltzTraP.save_interpolation","page":"Interpolation","text":"save_interpolation(filename, result::InterpolationResult)\nsave_interpolation(filename, interp::FourierInterpolator; kwargs...)\n\nSave interpolation results to file.\n\nSupported formats:\n\n.jld2: Julia native (HDF5-based, fast)\n.bt2: Python BoltzTraP2 compatible (JSON + LZMA)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.save_interpolation"},{"category":"function","location":"interpolate/#BoltzTraP.load_interpolation","page":"Interpolation","text":"load_interpolation(filename) -> InterpolationResult\n\nLoad interpolation results from file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.load_interpolation"},{"category":"section","location":"output_formats/#output-formats","page":"Output Formats","text":"BoltzTraP.jl produces result files in JLD2 format (Julia native) and supports Python BoltzTraP2's .bt2 format for compatibility.","title":"Output Formats"},{"category":"section","location":"output_formats/#Inspecting-Result-Files","page":"Output Formats","text":"Use the describe command to inspect result files:\n\nAPI usage:\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\n# Describe interpolation result\ninterp = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\")\ndescribe(interp)\n\n# Describe transport result\ntransport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures=[300.0])\ndescribe(transport)\n\n# Describe from file\ndescribe(\"si_interp.jld2\")\ndescribe(\"si_transport.jld2\")\n\nCLI usage:\n\n# Describe interpolation result\nboltztrap describe interpolation.jld2\n\n# Describe transport result\nboltztrap describe transport.jld2\n\n# Describe Python .bt2 file\nboltztrap describe python_result.bt2\n\n","title":"Inspecting Result Files"},{"category":"section","location":"output_formats/#Interpolation-Result-(.jld2)","page":"Output Formats","text":"Loading in Julia:\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\n# Using convenience function\nresult = load_interpolation(\"interpolation.jld2\")\n\n# Or using JLD2 directly\nusing JLD2\ndata = load(\"interpolation.jld2\")\ncoeffs = data[\"coeffs\"]\nequivalences = data[\"equivalences\"]\nfermi = data[\"metadata\"][\"fermi_energy\"]\n\nSaving:\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\ninterp = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\"; kpoints=5000)\nsave_interpolation(\"si_interp.jld2\", interp)\n\n","title":"Interpolation Result (.jld2)"},{"category":"section","location":"output_formats/#Transport-Result-(.jld2)","page":"Output Formats","text":"Loading in Julia:\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\n# Using convenience function\ntransport = load_integrate(\"transport.jld2\")\n\n# Access results\nprintln(\"Temperatures: \", transport.temperatures)\nprintln(\"Seebeck at 300K: \", transport.seebeck[1, 1, 1, :])\n\n# Or using JLD2 directly\nusing JLD2\ndata = load(\"transport.jld2\")\nsigma = data[\"sigma\"]      # 3x3 x nT x nmu\nseebeck = data[\"seebeck\"]  # 3x3 x nT x nmu\n\nSaving:\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\ntransport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures=[300.0])\nsave_integrate(\"si_transport.jld2\", transport)\n\n","title":"Transport Result (.jld2)"},{"category":"section","location":"output_formats/#CSV-Export","page":"Output Formats","text":"Transport results can be exported to CSV for external analysis:\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\ntransport = load_integrate(\"transport.jld2\")\nsave_integrate_csv(\"transport.csv\", transport)\n\nCSV columns:\n\nColumn Unit Description\nT K Temperature\nmu Ha Chemical potential\nN - Carrier concentration\nsigma_xx, sigma_yy, sigma_zz S/(m·s) σ/τ (electrical conductivity / relaxation time)\nS_xx, S_yy, S_zz V/K S (Seebeck coefficient)\nkappa_xx, kappa_yy, kappa_zz W/(m·K·s) κ₀/τ (electronic thermal conductivity / relaxation time)\n\n","title":"CSV Export"},{"category":"section","location":"output_formats/#Compatibility-with-Python-BoltzTraP2","page":"Output Formats","text":"BoltzTraP.jl supports reading and writing Python BoltzTraP2's .bt2 interpolation files, enabling seamless migration from existing Python workflows.\n\nOperation Python .bt2 Julia .jld2\nRead interpolation Supported Native\nWrite interpolation Supported Native (default)\nRead transport Not supported Native\nWrite transport Not supported Native\n\nUsage examples:\n\n# Read Python .bt2 and compute transport\nboltztrap integrate python_result.bt2 -t 300\n\n# Describe .bt2 file contents\nboltztrap describe python_result.bt2\n\n# Plot bands from .bt2 (requires --kpath for files without spacegroup)\nboltztrap plotbands python_result.bt2 --kpath \"G:0,0,0;X:0.5,0,0.5|G-X-G\"\n\n# Write interpolation in .bt2 format\nboltztrap interpolate ./Si.vasp -o si_interp.bt2\n\nFormat details:\n\nFormat Structure Use case\n.bt2 LZMA-compressed JSON Python compatibility\n.jld2 HDF5-based binary Julia native (recommended)\n\nnote: Transport results\nTransport calculation results (.btj in Python) are only available in .jld2 format. This is intentional as Python BoltzTraP2 also uses plain text formats (.trace, .condtens) for transport output.\n\n","title":"Compatibility with Python BoltzTraP2"},{"category":"section","location":"output_formats/#File-Size-Guidelines","page":"Output Formats","text":"Material k-points Interpolation Transport\nSi (2 atoms) 5000 ~1 MB ~5 MB\nComplex (50 atoms) 10000 ~50 MB ~20 MB\n\n","title":"File Size Guidelines"},{"category":"section","location":"output_formats/#Next-Steps","page":"Output Formats","text":"Input Formats - DFT input formats and loaders\nInterpolation - InterpolationResult usage\nIntegration - TransportResult usage","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"function","location":"output_formats/#BoltzTraP.describe","page":"Output Formats","text":"describe(file)\n\nDisplay information about a BoltzTraP.jl result file.\n\nShow basic information and metadata from interpolation (.jld2/.bt2) or transport result files (.jld2).\n\nArguments\n\nfile: Path to result file (.jld2 or .bt2)\n\nExamples\n\nboltztrap describe si_interp.jld2\nboltztrap describe si_interp.bt2\nboltztrap describe si_transport.jld2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.describe"},{"category":"type","location":"output_formats/#BoltzTraP.InterpolationResult","page":"Output Formats","text":"InterpolationResult\n\nContainer for interpolation results from run_interpolate.\n\nFields\n\ncoeffs: Fourier coefficients (nbands × neq)\nequivalences: Vector of equivalence class matrices\nlattvec: 3×3 lattice vectors (columns, in Bohr)\natoms: Atomic structure (positions, types) - optional\nmetadata: Dictionary with additional information\n\nMetadata Keys\n\nfermi_energy: Fermi energy in Ha\nnelect: Number of electrons\ndosweight: DOS weight (2.0 for non-spin-polarized, 1.0 for collinear)\nspintype: Spin type (\"Unpolarized\" or \"Collinear\")\nselected_bands: Band indices used (UnitRange)\nspacegroup_number: International space group number (1-230)\nspacegroup_symbol: Space group symbol (e.g., \"Fd-3m\")\nsource_file: Original DFT file path\ncreation_date: ISO 8601 timestamp\ngenerator: \"BoltzTraP.jl\"\n\nSee also: run_interpolate, run_integrate, save_interpolation, load_interpolation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.InterpolationResult"},{"category":"type","location":"output_formats/#BoltzTraP.TransportResult","page":"Output Formats","text":"TransportResult\n\nContainer for transport coefficients from run_integrate.\n\nContains transport coefficients and other quantities computed by BZ integration.\n\nFields\n\ntemperatures: Temperature array in Kelvin (K)\nmu_values: Chemical potential array in eV\nsigma: Electrical conductivity/τ tensor (3×3×nT×nμ)\nseebeck: Seebeck coefficient tensor (3×3×nT×nμ) in V/K\nkappa: Electronic thermal conductivity/τ tensor (3×3×nT×nμ)\ndos: Density of states data (optional Dict with epsilon, dos, vvdos)\nmetadata: Dictionary with additional information\n\nMetadata Keys\n\nfermi_energy: Fermi energy in Ha\nnelect: Number of electrons\ndosweight: DOS weight (2.0 for non-spin-polarized, 1.0 for collinear)\nspintype: Spin type (\"Unpolarized\" or \"Collinear\")\nsource_file: Original DFT file path\ncreation_date: ISO 8601 timestamp\nAdditional keys from interpolation step are preserved\n\nSee also: run_integrate, save_integrate, load_integrate\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.TransportResult"},{"category":"section","location":"reftest/#Reference-Tests","page":"Reference Tests","text":"BoltzTraP.jl provides reference tests (reftest) to validate numerical equivalence with Python BoltzTraP2.\n\nThese reference tests were used by the developers during implementation to ensure correctness. The same tests and data generation scripts are provided for users who wish to independently verify the implementation.","title":"Reference Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"reftest/#Overview","page":"Reference Tests","text":"Reference testing ensures BoltzTraP.jl produces results identical to the original Python implementation:\n\nAspect Description\nLocation reftest/ directory\nTolerance < 10⁻⁶ relative error\nTest count 80+ reference tests\nData source BoltzTraP2 v25.11.1","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"reftest/#What-is-Tested","page":"Reference Tests","text":"Reference tests validate numerical equivalence for:\n\nCategory What is Compared Tolerance\nSymmetry Equivalence classes, star functions, rotation matrices exact\nInterpolation Phase factors, Fourier coefficients, reconstructed bands < 10⁻¹⁰\nTransport DOS, transport DOS, Fermi integrals (L₀, L₁, L₂), Onsager coefficients (σ, S, κ) < 10⁻⁶\nI/O Lattice vectors, atomic positions, k-points, band energies, Fermi level exact\nEnd-to-end Full workflow from DFT data to transport coefficients < 10⁻⁶","title":"What is Tested"},{"category":"section","location":"reftest/#Test-Materials","page":"Reference Tests","text":"Material Structure Type Purpose\nSi Diamond (Fd-3m) Semiconductor High-symmetry cubic\nPbTe Rock salt (Fm-3m) Thermoelectric Heavy elements\nFe BCC (Im-3m) Ferromagnetic metal Collinear magnetic\nSynthetic Monoclinic, Triclinic - Low-symmetry validation","title":"Test Materials"},{"category":"section","location":"reftest/#Test-Categories","page":"Reference Tests","text":"","title":"Test Categories"},{"category":"section","location":"reftest/#Symmetry-and-Equivalences","page":"Reference Tests","text":"Tests for k-point symmetry and equivalence class generation.\n\nTest Material Description\nunit_cube Generic Unit cube lattice vectors\nsimple_cubic Generic Simple cubic with atoms\nsi_diamond Si Diamond structure symmetry\nfe_bcc_magnetic Fe BCC with magnetic moments\nmonoclinic Generic Monoclinic space group\ntriclinic_p1 Generic P1 (no symmetry)","title":"Symmetry and Equivalences"},{"category":"section","location":"reftest/#Band-Interpolation","page":"Reference Tests","text":"Tests for Fourier coefficient fitting and band reconstruction.\n\nTest Material k-points Bands Equivalences\nsi_interpolation Si 165 6 1,102","title":"Band Interpolation"},{"category":"section","location":"reftest/#Transport-Coefficients","page":"Reference Tests","text":"Tests for Fermi integrals and Onsager coefficients.\n\nTest Material Temperatures Chemical potentials\nsimple_transport Si 200, 300, 400 K 50 points","title":"Transport Coefficients"},{"category":"section","location":"reftest/#File-I/O","page":"Reference Tests","text":"Tests for reading DFT output files.\n\nFormat Materials Test Count\nVASP Si, PbTe 18\nQE Si, Fe, CrI3 27\nWien2k Si, CoSb3, Bi2Te3 27\nGENE Si, Synthetic 18\nABINIT Si 9","title":"File I/O"},{"category":"section","location":"reftest/#End-to-End-Tests","page":"Reference Tests","text":"Complete workflow tests from DFT data to transport coefficients.\n\nTest Material Description\nsi_end2end Si Full Si workflow\npbte_end2end PbTe Full PbTe workflow\nfe_collinear_e2e Fe Full Fe collinear workflow","title":"End-to-End Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"reftest/#Running-Reference-Tests","page":"Reference Tests","text":"For instructions on running reference tests (prerequisites, data generation, execution), see:\n\nreftest/README.md","title":"Running Reference Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"reftest/#See-Also","page":"Reference Tests","text":"Validation - Visual comparison with Python results\nBenchmarks - Performance comparison","title":"See Also"},{"category":"section","location":"workflow/#API-Workflow","page":"API Workflow","text":"This page describes the API workflow - using BoltzTraP.jl as a Julia library.\n\ninfo: CLI vs API Workflow\nCLI workflow: Run calculations via boltztrap shell command\nAPI workflow: Call Julia functions (run_interpolate, run_integrate) directlyFor CLI workflow, see CLI Workflow.\n\nBoltzTraP.jl supports multiple input methods for transport calculations:\n\nMethod Input Loader\nVASP vasprun.xml load_vasp\nQuantum ESPRESSO data-file-schema.xml load_qe\nWien2k case.struct, case.energy load_wien2k\nGENE/Generic case.structure, case.energy load_gene\nABINIT *_GSR.nc (NetCDF) load_abinit\nDFTK.jl scfres from DFTK.jl load_dftk\n\nSee Input Formats for detailed format specifications.\n\n","title":"API Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"workflow/#Overview","page":"API Workflow","text":"All workflows follow the same pattern:\n\n┌───────────────────────────────┐\n│         Data Source           │\n│ (VASP/QE/Wien2k/GENE/ABINIT/  │\n│  DFTK)                        │\n└───────────────┬───────────────┘\n                │ load_*() → DFTData\n                ▼\n┌───────────────────────────────┐\n│       run_interpolate         │\n│        (Fourier fit)          │\n└───────────────┬───────────────┘\n                │\n                ▼\n        InterpolationResult\n                │\n                ▼\n┌───────────────────────────────┐\n│        run_integrate          │\n│    (transport coefficients)   │\n└───────────────┬───────────────┘\n                │\n                ▼\n         TransportResult\n\nrun_integrate accepts both InterpolationResult directly or a file path:\n\n# Direct (no intermediate file)\n# Note: \"./Si.vasp\" is a directory containing vasprun.xml and POSCAR\ninterp = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\")\ntransport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures=[300.0])\n\n# Via file (for later reuse)\ninterp = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\")\nsave_interpolation(\"si_interp.jld2\", interp)\ntransport = run_integrate(\"si_interp.jld2\"; temperatures=[300.0])\n\n","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"workflow/#VASP-Workflow","page":"API Workflow","text":"","title":"VASP Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"workflow/#Step-1:-Interpolate","page":"API Workflow","text":"run_interpolate reads DFT data and fits band energies to a Fourier series.\n\ninterp = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\"; kpoints=5000, verbose=true)","title":"Step 1: Interpolate"},{"category":"section","location":"workflow/#Step-2:-Integrate","page":"API Workflow","text":"run_integrate computes transport coefficients by BZ integration.\n\ntransport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures=[300.0], verbose=true)","title":"Step 2: Integrate"},{"category":"section","location":"workflow/#Complete-Example","page":"API Workflow","text":"using BoltzTraP\n\n# Direct workflow (no intermediate files)\ninterp = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\"; kpoints=5000, verbose=true)\ntransport = run_integrate(interp;\n    temperatures = collect(100:100:800),\n    verbose = true\n)\n\n# Optional: save results for later\nsave_interpolation(\"si_interp.jld2\", interp)\nsave_integrate(\"si_transport.jld2\", transport)\n\n# Analyze results\nusing Plots\nT_idx = findfirst(==(300.0), transport.temperatures)\nS_xx = transport.seebeck[1, 1, T_idx, :] .* 1e6  # V/K -> uV/K\nplot(transport.mu_values, S_xx, xlabel=\"mu (eV)\", ylabel=\"S_xx (uV/K)\")\n\n","title":"Complete Example"},{"category":"section","location":"workflow/#QE-Workflow","page":"API Workflow","text":"Similar to VASP, using load_qe:\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\n# Load QE data\ndata = load_qe(\"./Si.qe\")\n\n# Run interpolation\ninterp = run_interpolate(data; source=\"./Si.qe\", kpoints=5000, verbose=true)\n\n# Compute transport\ntransport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures=[300.0])\n\n","title":"QE Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"workflow/#DFTK-Workflow","page":"API Workflow","text":"See DFTK.jl Integration for detailed documentation.\n\n","title":"DFTK Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"workflow/#Generic-Data-Workflow","page":"API Workflow","text":"For other data sources, prepare a NamedTuple with the required fields:\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\n# Prepare data (all in atomic units: Hartree, Bohr)\ndata = (\n    lattice = ...,       # 3x3 matrix, columns are lattice vectors (Bohr)\n    positions = ...,     # 3xN matrix, fractional coordinates\n    species = [...],     # Vector of element symbols\n    kpoints = ...,       # 3xM matrix, fractional coordinates\n    weights = ...,       # M-vector, k-point weights\n    ebands = ...,        # (nbands, M, nspin) array (Hartree)\n    occupations = ...,   # (nbands, M, nspin) array\n    fermi = ...,         # Fermi energy (Hartree)\n    nelect = ...,        # Number of electrons\n)\n\ninterp = run_interpolate(data; source=\"custom\", kpoints=5000)\ntransport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures=[300.0])\n\n","title":"Generic Data Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"workflow/#Utility-Functions","page":"API Workflow","text":"","title":"Utility Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"workflow/#Format-Detection","page":"API Workflow","text":"detected_format auto-detects the DFT format in a directory:\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\nformat = detected_format(\"./calculation\")\nprintln(\"Detected: $format\")  # \"VASP\", \"QE\", \"Wien2k\", \"GENE\", \"ABINIT\", or nothing\n\nThis is used internally by run_interpolate and load_dft for auto-detection.\n\n","title":"Format Detection"},{"category":"section","location":"workflow/#Next-Steps","page":"API Workflow","text":"Interpolation - run_interpolate details\nIntegration - run_integrate details\nUtilities - calc_cv and other utility functions\nPlotting - Visualization functions\nInput Formats - DFT input formats and loaders\nOutput Formats - Result file formats\nConventions - Units and array conventions","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Validation","page":"Validation","text":"This page explains how BoltzTraP.jl was validated against Python BoltzTraP2, and how you can verify the results yourself.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Overview","page":"Validation","text":"BoltzTraP.jl is a faithful Julia port of Python BoltzTraP2. All transport coefficients (electrical conductivity σ, Seebeck coefficient S, thermal conductivity κ) match within numerical precision (< 10⁻⁶ relative error).","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#How-BoltzTraP.jl-Was-Validated","page":"Validation","text":"","title":"How BoltzTraP.jl Was Validated"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Reference-Testing-Methodology","page":"Validation","text":"BoltzTraP.jl was developed using reference testing - a methodology where:\n\nPython BoltzTraP2 computes reference outputs for various inputs\nJulia BoltzTraP.jl computes the same quantities from the same inputs\nResults are compared to verify numerical equivalence\n\nThis approach guarantees that BoltzTraP.jl produces results identical to the original Python implementation.","title":"Reference Testing Methodology"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Validation-Summary","page":"Validation","text":"Aspect Details\nTest cases 727 (5004 assertions)\nTolerance < 10⁻⁶ relative error\nData source BoltzTraP2 v25.11.1 (PyPI)\nMaterials tested Si, PbTe, Fe (collinear)","title":"Validation Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Reference-Data-Source","page":"Validation","text":"All reference data originates from the test materials included in the BoltzTraP2 package (v25.11.1). These DFT calculations were performed by the BoltzTraP2 developers and are distributed with the package for testing purposes.\n\nRepository and Citation:\n\nGitLab: https://gitlab.com/souza-group/BoltzTraP2\nPyPI: https://pypi.org/project/BoltzTraP2/\nPaper: Madsen, G. K., Carrete, J., & Verstraete, M. J. (2018). BoltzTraP2, a program for interpolating band structures and calculating semi-classical transport coefficients. Computer Physics Communications, 231, 140-145. doi:10.1016/j.cpc.2018.05.010\n\nDFT Calculation Details:\n\nMaterial Structure DFT Code XC Functional k-grid Pseudopotential\nSi Diamond (Fd-3m) VASP PBE 17×17×17 PAW_PBE Si\nPbTe Rock salt (Fm-3m) VASP PBE 16×16×16 PAWPBE Pbd, Te\nFe BCC (Im-3m) QE PBE - ONCV\n\nData Provenance and Verification:\n\nFor package checksums, test data checksums, and verification instructions, see reftest/README.md.","title":"Reference Data Source"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Components-Tested","page":"Validation","text":"Component Test Cases Description\nSymmetry 37 Space group detection, rotations, equivalences\nTypes 46 DFTData, SpinTypes, BandData\nInterpolation 57 Fourier coefficient fitting, reconstruction\nTransport 31 Fermi integrals, Onsager coefficients\nI/O 130 VASP/QE file parsing\nLoaders 92 Multi-format loader comparison\nWorkflow 63 End-to-end magnetic validation","title":"Components Tested"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Reference-Data-Scripts","page":"Validation","text":"All reference data is generated from Python BoltzTraP2 using scripts in the reftest/ directory. See reftest/README.md for complete details.\n\nDirectory structure:\n\nreftest/\n├── README.md                         # Complete usage instructions\n├── common.py                         # Shared utilities for generators\n├── compare.jl                        # Julia comparison script\n├── convert_dft_to_gene.py            # DFT → GENE format converter\n├── generate_0_unit_test_data.py      # [0] Unit test fixtures (test/data/)\n├── generate_1_sphere.py              # [1] Sphere, equivalences, symmetry\n├── generate_2_interpolation_si.py    # [2] Interpolation (Si, cubic)\n├── generate_3_transport.py           # [3] Transport coefficients\n├── generate_4_io_poscar.py           # [4] POSCAR I/O\n├── generate_4_io_bt2.py              # [4] BT2 file format\n├── generate_5_e2e.py                 # [5] End-to-end (Si, Li, PbTe)\n├── generate_6_loaders.py             # [6] DFT loader validation\n├── generate_synthetic_lowsym.py      # [7] Synthetic low-symmetry test data\n├── generate_synthetic_p1.py          # [7] Synthetic P1 triclinic data\n└── data/                             # Generated .npz files (gitignored)\n    ├── si_interpolation.npz\n    ├── si_end2end.npz\n    ├── pbte_end2end.npz\n    └── ...\n\nKey scripts:\n\nCategory Script Description\n0 generate_0_unit_test_data.py Unit test fixtures\n1 generate_1_sphere.py Symmetry and equivalences\n2 generate_2_interpolation_*.py Interpolation coefficients\n3 generate_3_transport.py Transport coefficients\n4 generate_4_io_*.py File I/O (POSCAR, BT2)\n5 generate_5_e2e.py End-to-end workflows\n6 generate_6_loaders.py DFT loader validation (VASP, QE, Wien2k, GENE, ABINIT)\n7 generate_synthetic_*.py Synthetic low-symmetry test data\n- compare.jl Julia comparison against reference data","title":"Reference Data Scripts"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Verify-It-Yourself","page":"Validation","text":"You can reproduce the validation using the tools provided in the reftest/ and validation/ directories.","title":"Verify It Yourself"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Prerequisites","page":"Validation","text":"","title":"Prerequisites"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#1.-Install-Python-BoltzTraP2","page":"Validation","text":"pip install boltztrap2\n\nOr from source:\n\ngit clone https://gitlab.com/souza-group/BoltzTraP2.git\ncd BoltzTraP2\npip install -e .","title":"1. Install Python BoltzTraP2"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#2.-Prepare-Test-Data","page":"Validation","text":"BoltzTraP2 includes test materials. After installation, locate the data directory:\n\n# Find BoltzTraP2 installation\npython -c \"import BoltzTraP2; print(BoltzTraP2.__path__[0])\"\n\n# Test data is in the 'data' subdirectory:\n#   BoltzTraP2/data/Si.vasp/\n#   BoltzTraP2/data/PbTe.vasp.unpolarized/","title":"2. Prepare Test Data"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Quick-Validation","page":"Validation","text":"","title":"Quick Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Step-1:-Generate-Python-Reference-Data","page":"Validation","text":"cd BoltzTraP.jl/reftest\n\n# Generate all reference data\npython generate_1_sphere.py\npython generate_2_interpolation_si.py\npython generate_3_transport.py\npython generate_4_io_poscar.py\npython generate_4_io_bt2.py\npython generate_5_e2e.py\n\nThis creates .npz files in reftest/data/ containing Python-computed results:\n\nsi_end2end.npz - Si transport coefficients\npbte_end2end.npz - PbTe transport coefficients\nAnd others for intermediate validation","title":"Step 1: Generate Python Reference Data"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Step-2:-Run-Automated-Comparison","page":"Validation","text":"cd BoltzTraP.jl/reftest\n\n# Run all comparisons\njulia compare.jl\n\n# Or run specific tests\njulia compare.jl si_diamond      # Symmetry test\njulia compare.jl transport       # Transport test\njulia compare.jl integrate_e2e   # End-to-end test","title":"Step 2: Run Automated Comparison"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Step-3:-Generate-Julia-Transport-Results","page":"Validation","text":"Generate Julia transport results from the same input data:\n\ncd BoltzTraP.jl\n\n# Generate Si transport (from VASP data in reftest)\njulia --project -e '\nusing BoltzTraP, NPZ\ndata = npzread(\"reftest/data/vasp_si.npz\")\ndft_data = (\n    lattice = data[\"lattvec\"],\n    positions = transpose(data[\"positions\"]),\n    species = [\"Si\", \"Si\"],\n    kpoints = transpose(data[\"kpoints\"]),\n    ebands = data[\"ebands\"],\n    fermi = data[\"fermi\"],\n    nelect = data[\"nelect\"],\n    dosweight = data[\"dosweight\"],\n)\ninterp = run_interpolate(dft_data; source=\"VASP\", kpoints=5000)\ntransport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures=[300.0])\nsave_integrate(\"si_transport.jld2\", transport)\n'\n\n# Similarly for PbTe\njulia --project -e '\nusing BoltzTraP, NPZ\ndata = npzread(\"reftest/data/vasp_pbte.npz\")\ndft_data = (\n    lattice = data[\"lattvec\"],\n    positions = transpose(data[\"positions\"]),\n    species = [\"Pb\", \"Te\"],\n    kpoints = transpose(data[\"kpoints\"]),\n    ebands = data[\"ebands\"],\n    fermi = data[\"fermi\"],\n    nelect = data[\"nelect\"],\n    dosweight = data[\"dosweight\"],\n)\ninterp = run_interpolate(dft_data; source=\"VASP\", kpoints=5000)\ntransport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures=[300.0])\nsave_integrate(\"pbte_transport.jld2\", transport)\n'","title":"Step 3: Generate Julia Transport Results"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Step-4:-Visual-Comparison","page":"Validation","text":"Compare Python and Julia results visually:\n\ncd BoltzTraP.jl\n\n# Compare Si transport coefficients\njulia --project validation/compare_transport.jl \\\n    reftest/data/si_end2end.npz \\\n    si_transport.jld2 \\\n    --title Si\n\n# Compare PbTe transport coefficients\njulia --project validation/compare_transport.jl \\\n    reftest/data/pbte_end2end.npz \\\n    pbte_transport.jld2 \\\n    --title PbTe\n\nOptions:\n\nOption Description Default\n--title <name> Material name for figure title untitled\n--xlims <min,max> X-axis limits in eV -0.5,0.5\n-t, --temperature <T> Temperature in K 300\n--alltemp Generate figures for all common temperatures -\n-o, --output <file> Output figure path validation/transport_<title>_<T>K.png","title":"Step 4: Visual Comparison"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Step-5:-View-Results","page":"Validation","text":"Comparison figures are saved in validation/:\n\nFile Description\ntransport_Si_300K.png 3-panel comparison for Si at 300K (S, σ, κ)\ntransport_PbTe_300K.png 3-panel comparison for PbTe at 300K (S, σ, κ)","title":"Step 5: View Results"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Available-Materials","page":"Validation","text":"Material Type Description\nSi Semiconductor Silicon (diamond structure)\nPbTe Thermoelectric Lead telluride\nFe Ferromagnetic metal BCC Iron (collinear, T=1000K)","title":"Available Materials"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Available-Properties","page":"Validation","text":"Property Symbol Units\nSeebeck coefficient S V/K\nElectrical conductivity σ S/m (divided by τ)\nThermal conductivity κ W/(m·K) (divided by τ)","title":"Available Properties"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Detailed-Validation","page":"Validation","text":"For more comprehensive validation including intermediate quantities (Fourier coefficients, DOS, Fermi integrals), see the Reference Tests page.","title":"Detailed Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Expected-Results","page":"Validation","text":"When validation is successful, you should see:\n\nVisual overlap: Python (dashed) and Julia (solid) curves should be indistinguishable\nNumerical agreement: Relative differences < 10⁻⁶\n\nExample output:\n\nComparing S for Si at T=300K\n  Python: S_xx = 6.575e-06 V/K\n  Julia:  S_xx = 6.575e-06 V/K\n  Relative difference: 2.3e-10\n  ✓ PASS","title":"Expected Results"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Troubleshooting","page":"Validation","text":"","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#\"Reference-data-not-found\"","page":"Validation","text":"Generate the reference data first:\n\ncd reftest\npython generate_5_e2e.py","title":"\"Reference data not found\""},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#\"BoltzTraP2-module-not-found\"","page":"Validation","text":"Install Python BoltzTraP2:\n\npip install boltztrap2","title":"\"BoltzTraP2 module not found\""},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Large-numerical-differences","page":"Validation","text":"If differences exceed 10⁻⁶:\n\nCheck BoltzTraP2 version (v25.11.1 recommended)\nEnsure reference data was regenerated after any BoltzTraP2 update\nReport an issue on GitHub with details","title":"Large numerical differences"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#See-Also","page":"Validation","text":"Reference Tests - Technical details of reference tests (for developers extending tests)\nBenchmarks - Performance comparison with Python","title":"See Also"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Plotting","page":"Plotting","text":"BoltzTraP.jl provides plotting via package extensions:\n\nExtension Trigger Functionality\nRecipesBaseExt using RecipesBase (or any backend) plot(data) recipes for BandPlotData / TransportPlotData\nPlotsExt using Plots plot_bands, plot_transport (full-featured with auto k-path)\nPythonPlotExt using PythonPlot savefig_publication for publication-quality PDF/PNG/SVG figures\n\n","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#RecipesBase-Recipes","page":"Plotting","text":"These recipes are activated when any RecipesBase-compatible backend is loaded.\n\nusing BoltzTraP, Plots   # or CairoMakie, etc.","title":"RecipesBase Recipes"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Band-Structure-—-BandPlotData","page":"Plotting","text":"BandPlotData is a backend-independent container created internally by plot_bands.\n\ninterp = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\"; kpoints=5000)\nbpd = BandPlotData(interp)  # created internally by plot_bands\nplot(bpd)\n\nThe recipe draws band lines, a Fermi level (red dashed), and vertical lines at high-symmetry points.\n\nAttribute Default\nylabel \"Energy (eV)\"\nylims (emin, emax)\nxticks high-symmetry labels\nframestyle :box\nlinecolor :black\nlinewidth 1.5","title":"Band Structure — BandPlotData"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Transport-Coefficients-—-TransportPlotData","page":"Plotting","text":"transport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures=[300.0])\ntpd = TransportPlotData(transport)  # created internally by plot_transport\nplot(tpd)\n\nAttribute Default\nxlabel from data\nylabel from data\ntitle from data\nlinewidth 2","title":"Transport Coefficients — TransportPlotData"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Customization","page":"Plotting","text":"Override any attribute with standard keyword arguments:\n\nplot(bpd, ylabel=\"E − E_F (eV)\", linecolor=:blue, size=(800, 600))\n\n","title":"Customization"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Plots.jl-Convenience-Functions","page":"Plotting","text":"note: Requires Plots.jl\nplot_bands and plot_transport require using Plots. They internally create BandPlotData / TransportPlotData and provide additional features such as automatic k-path generation via Brillouin.jl.","title":"Plots.jl Convenience Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Band-Structure-Plotting","page":"Plotting","text":"plot_bands plots interpolated band structure along high-symmetry k-paths.","title":"Band Structure Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#From-InterpolationResult","page":"Plotting","text":"using BoltzTraP\n\ninterp = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\"; kpoints=5000)\nplot_bands(interp; emin=-5.0, emax=5.0, output=\"bands.png\")","title":"From InterpolationResult"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#From-File","page":"Plotting","text":"# From .jld2 (auto k-path from spacegroup metadata)\nplot_bands(\"si_interp.jld2\"; emin=-5.0, emax=5.0, output=\"bands.png\")\n\n# From .bt2 (manual k-path required if no spacegroup)\nkpath = (\n    points = Dict(\"G\" => [0,0,0], \"X\" => [0.5,0,0.5], \"L\" => [0.5,0.5,0.5]),\n    paths = [[\"G\", \"X\", \"L\", \"G\"]]\n)\nplot_bands(\"si_interp.bt2\"; kpath=kpath, emin=-5.0, emax=5.0)","title":"From File"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Options","page":"Plotting","text":"Option Type Default Description\nnpoints Int 100 K-points per path segment\nemin Float64 -1.0 Minimum energy relative to Fermi [eV]\nemax Float64 1.0 Maximum energy relative to Fermi [eV]\nfermi_line Bool true Show Fermi level as dashed line\nkpath NamedTuple nothing Manual k-path specification\noutput String nothing Save to file if specified","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Manual-K-path-Format","page":"Plotting","text":"For files without spacegroup metadata (e.g., Python .bt2 files), specify k-path manually:\n\nkpath = (\n    points = Dict(\n        \"G\" => [0.0, 0.0, 0.0],      # Gamma\n        \"X\" => [0.5, 0.0, 0.5],      # X\n        \"L\" => [0.5, 0.5, 0.5],      # L\n        \"W\" => [0.5, 0.25, 0.75],    # W\n    ),\n    paths = [[\"G\", \"X\", \"W\", \"L\", \"G\"]]  # Path segments\n)\n\n","title":"Manual K-path Format"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Transport-Coefficient-Plotting","page":"Plotting","text":"plot_transport plots transport coefficients vs chemical potential or temperature.","title":"Transport Coefficient Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#From-TransportResult","page":"Plotting","text":"using BoltzTraP\n\ninterp = run_interpolate(\"./Si.vasp\"; kpoints=5000)\ntransport = run_integrate(interp; temperatures=[300.0])\n\n# Seebeck vs chemical potential\nplot_transport(transport; quantity=\"seebeck\", component=\"xx\", output=\"seebeck.png\")\n\n# Conductivity vs temperature\nplot_transport(transport; quantity=\"sigma\", abscissa=\"T\", output=\"sigma_T.png\")","title":"From TransportResult"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#From-File-2","page":"Plotting","text":"plot_transport(\"si_transport.jld2\"; quantity=\"seebeck\", component=\"xx\")\nplot_transport(\"si_transport.jld2\"; quantity=\"sigma\", abscissa=\"T\")","title":"From File"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Options-2","page":"Plotting","text":"Option Type Default Description\nquantity String \"seebeck\" Property: seebeck, sigma, kappa\ncomponent String \"xx\" Tensor component: xx, yy, zz, xy, ...\nabscissa String \"mu\" X-axis: mu (chemical potential) or T (temperature)\ntemperature Float64 300.0 Temperature for mu plot [K]\nmu_index Int 0 mu index for T plot (0 = auto-select near Fermi)\noutput String nothing Save to file if specified","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Available-Quantities","page":"Plotting","text":"Quantity Description Units\nseebeck or S Seebeck coefficient uV/K\nsigma or conductivity Electrical conductivity / tau S/m/s\nkappa or thermal Thermal conductivity / tau W/m/K/s\n\n","title":"Available Quantities"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Publication-quality-Figures-with-PythonPlot","page":"Plotting","text":"savefig_publication writes publication-quality PDF, PNG, or SVG figures via PythonPlot.jl. Loading PythonPlot activates BoltzTraPPythonPlotExt, which handles matplotlib through CondaPkg/PythonCall (no manual matplotlib install required).","title":"Publication-quality Figures with PythonPlot"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Builder:-build_transport_plot_data","page":"Plotting","text":"build_transport_plot_data extracts a TransportPlotData from a TransportResult, performing label formatting, unit conversion, and input validation in one place. The same TransportPlotData is consumed by the RecipesBase recipe, plot_transport (Plots), and savefig_publication (PythonPlot), so all backends render identical data and labels.\n\nusing BoltzTraP\n\ntransport = load_integrate(\"si_transport.jld2\")\n\ntpd = build_transport_plot_data(transport;\n    quantity = \"seebeck\",       # \"seebeck\"/\"S\", \"sigma\"/\"conductivity\", \"kappa\"/\"thermal\"\n    component = \"xx\",           # xx, yy, zz, xy, xz, yz, yx, zx, zy\n    abscissa = \"mu\",            # \"mu\" or \"T\"\n    temperature = 300.0,        # K — used when abscissa = \"mu\"\n)\n\nbuild_transport_plot_data raises ArgumentError for unknown quantity, component, or abscissa values, and when the requested temperature is not present in transport.temperatures.","title":"Builder: build_transport_plot_data"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#savefig_publication","page":"Plotting","text":"using BoltzTraP, PythonPlot\n\n# Single panel (PDF inferred from the file extension)\nsavefig_publication(tpd, \"seebeck.pdf\";\n    axis_width_cm = 8.0, axis_height_cm = 5.0)\n\n# 1×3 subplot grid for S, σ, κ vs μ at T = 300 K\ntpds = [\n    build_transport_plot_data(transport;\n        quantity = q, component = \"xx\", abscissa = \"mu\", temperature = 300.0)\n    for q in (\"seebeck\", \"sigma\", \"kappa\")\n]\nsavefig_publication(tpds, \"transport_combined.pdf\";\n    axis_width_cm = 6.0, axis_height_cm = 4.5,\n    layout = (1, 3))\n\nLayout, margins, and gaps are specified in centimeters; the figure size in inches is derived internally. Output format is inferred from the file extension (.pdf, .png, .svg, ...).","title":"savefig_publication"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Demo:-Si-transport-at-T-300-K","page":"Plotting","text":"The figures below were produced from examples/110_si_vasp_pbe_paw.jl followed by examples/119_si_vasp_pbe_paw_publication.jl:\n\nCombined S/σ/κ vs μ (1×3 layout)\nSeebeck vs μ (single panel)","title":"Demo: Si transport at T = 300 K"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Band-figures","page":"Plotting","text":"savefig_publication(::BandPlotData, path; ...) is also provided. Construct a BandPlotData from interpolated band energies and a k-path before calling it; an end-to-end builder for band data is planned for a future release.\n\n","title":"Band figures"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#CLI-Commands","page":"Plotting","text":"For command-line usage, see CLI Workflow:\n\n# Band structure\nboltztrap plotbands si_interp.jld2 --emin -5 --emax 5 -o bands.png\nboltztrap plotbands si_interp.bt2 --kpath \"G:0,0,0;X:0.5,0,0.5|G-X-G\" -o bands.png\n\n# Transport coefficients\nboltztrap plot si_transport.jld2 -q seebeck -c xx -o seebeck.png\nboltztrap plot si_transport.jld2 -q sigma --abscissa T -o sigma_T.png\n\n","title":"CLI Commands"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#API-Reference","page":"Plotting","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"function","location":"plotting/#BoltzTraP.plot_bands","page":"Plotting","text":"plot_bands(result::InterpolationResult; kwargs...)\nplot_bands(file::String; kwargs...)\n\nPlot band structure. Requires using Plots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.plot_bands"},{"category":"function","location":"plotting/#BoltzTraP.plot_transport","page":"Plotting","text":"plot_transport(result::TransportResult; kwargs...)\nplot_transport(file::String; kwargs...)\n\nPlot transport properties. Requires using Plots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.plot_transport"},{"category":"function","location":"plotting/#BoltzTraP.build_transport_plot_data","page":"Plotting","text":"build_transport_plot_data(result::TransportResult;\n    quantity=\"seebeck\", component=\"xx\", abscissa=\"mu\",\n    temperature=300.0, mu_index=0) -> TransportPlotData\n\nBuild a TransportPlotData from a TransportResult for plotting via any backend (RecipesBase recipe, Plots, PythonPlot). Performs label formatting, unit conversion, and input validation in a single place.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nquantity::AbstractString = \"seebeck\" — \"seebeck\"/\"S\", \"sigma\"/\"conductivity\", or \"kappa\"/\"thermal\".\ncomponent::AbstractString = \"xx\" — Tensor component (xx, yy, zz, xy, xz, yz, yx, zx, zy).\nabscissa::AbstractString = \"mu\" — \"mu\" for vs chemical potential, \"T\" for vs temperature.\ntemperature::Float64 = 300.0 — Temperature [K] used when abscissa = \"mu\".\nmu_index::Int = 0 — μ-grid index used when abscissa = \"T\". 0 selects the value closest to the Fermi level.\n\nThrows ArgumentError for unknown quantity, component, or abscissa, or when temperature is not present in result.temperatures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.build_transport_plot_data"},{"category":"function","location":"plotting/#BoltzTraP.savefig_publication","page":"Plotting","text":"savefig_publication(bpd::BandPlotData, path; kwargs...)\nsavefig_publication(bpds::AbstractVector{<:BandPlotData}, path; kwargs...)\nsavefig_publication(tpd::TransportPlotData, path; kwargs...)\nsavefig_publication(tpds::AbstractVector{<:TransportPlotData}, path; kwargs...)\n\nSave a publication-quality figure via the PythonPlot extension. Requires using PythonPlot to activate BoltzTraPPythonPlotExt.\n\nThe output format is inferred from the file extension (.pdf, .png, .svg, ...).\n\nKeyword arguments\n\naxis_width_cm = 8.0, axis_height_cm = 6.0 — Axis size in centimeters.\nylims = nothing — Y-axis range (auto if nothing; for BandPlotData defaults to (emin, emax)).\ntitle = \"\" — Plot title (suppressed if empty; falls back to data.title).\nlayout = (1, length(data)) — Subplot grid for the vector overloads.\noverlay = false — Plot all bpds on a single axis (vector BandPlotData overload only).\n\nSee also: plot_bands, plot_transport.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BoltzTraP.savefig_publication"},{"category":"section","location":"#BoltzTraP.jl","page":"Home","text":"Julia implementation of BoltzTraP2, a band structure interpolation and semi-classical transport coefficient calculator.","title":"BoltzTraP.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"info: BoltzTraP.jl provides two workflows\nCLI workflow (Command Line): Run from terminal with boltztrap command. No programming required.\nAPI workflow (Julia Session): Call functions from Julia REPL or scripts. Suitable for customization and automation.Both workflows produce identical results.","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Available-Commands","page":"Home","text":"Feature CLI API Input Output\nBand interpolation boltztrap interpolate run_interpolate() VASP, QE, Wien2k, GENE, ABINIT, DFTK (scfres) InterpolationResult (.jld2, .bt2)\nTransport calculation boltztrap integrate run_integrate() InterpolationResult (.jld2, .bt2) TransportResult (.jld2)\nPlot bands boltztrap plotbands plot_bands() InterpolationResult (.jld2, .bt2) PNG, PDF\nPlot transport boltztrap plot plot_transport() TransportResult (.jld2) PNG, PDF\nDescribe results boltztrap describe describe() InterpolationResult or TransportResult -\n\nSee CLI Workflow and API Workflow for detailed usage. For file format details, see Input Formats and Output Formats.","title":"Available Commands"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"Home","text":"# 1. Interpolate band structure from VASP data\nboltztrap interpolate ./Si.vasp -v\n# Output: Si.vasp_interp.jld2\n\n# 2. Compute transport coefficients at 300K\nboltztrap integrate Si.vasp_interp.jld2 -t 300 -v\n# Output: Si.vasp_interp_transport.jld2\n\n# 3. Plot Seebeck coefficient\nboltztrap plot Si.vasp_interp_transport.jld2 -q seebeck -o seebeck.png\n\nFor detailed CLI usage, see CLI Workflow. For Julia API, see API Workflow.","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Scope-and-Limitations","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Scope and Limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"#Supported-Materials","page":"Home","text":"Material Type Support Notes\nNon-magnetic ✅ Full Default (dosweight=2)\nCollinear magnetic ✅ Full Spin-polarized (dosweight=1)\nNon-collinear magnetic ⬜ Planned v0.4+","title":"Supported Materials"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features-beyond-Python-BoltzTraP2","page":"Home","text":"Julia API: Programmatic workflow with run_interpolate() and run_integrate() functions\nDFTK.jl integration: Direct coupling with Julia-native DFT package via load_dftk()\nWannier.jl integration: Optional Wannier-basis interpolation path (v0.4+); see Wannier.jl Integration\nJLD2 format: Fast HDF5-based native Julia format for result files","title":"Features beyond Python BoltzTraP2"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"Requirements: Julia 1.10 or later\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"BoltzTraP\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#cli-setup","page":"Home","text":"For command-line usage (boltztrap command), additional setup is required:\n\nOption 1: Shell Alias (Recommended)\n\nAdd to ~/.bashrc or ~/.zshrc:\n\nalias boltztrap='julia -e \"using BoltzTraP; BoltzTraP.command_main(ARGS)\" --'\n\nOption 2: Install Binary\n\nusing BoltzTraP\nBoltzTraP.comonicon_install()  # Installs to ~/.julia/bin/\n\nThen add to PATH: export PATH=\"$HOME/.julia/bin:$PATH\"\n\nnote: Note\nFor API usage (run_interpolate, run_integrate), no additional setup is needed.","title":"CLI Setup (Optional)"},{"category":"section","location":"#Compatibility-with-Python-BoltzTraP2","page":"Home","text":"BoltzTraP.jl aims for numerical compatibility with Python BoltzTraP2. To ensure bit-for-bit compatibility with the reference implementation, BoltzTraP.jl uses explicit numerical constants for all unit conversions, avoiding potential discrepancies from unit libraries.\n\nKey differences:\n\nJulia uses atomic units (Hartree) internally, consistent with DFTK.jl\nDefault output format is JLD2 (Julia native); .bt2 format also supported for Python compatibility","title":"Compatibility with Python BoltzTraP2"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citation","page":"Home","text":"If you use BoltzTraP.jl in your research, please cite the following:\n\nBoltzTraP.jl:\n\nSugawara, H. (2026). BoltzTraP.jl: Julia port of BoltzTraP2 for band structure interpolation and transport coefficient calculation (Version 0.3.4) [Computer software]. doi:10.5281/zenodo.20255943\n\nOriginal BoltzTraP2:\n\nMadsen, G. K., Carrete, J., & Verstraete, M. J. (2018). BoltzTraP2, a program for interpolating band structures and calculating semi-classical transport coefficients. Computer Physics Communications, 231, 140-145. doi:10.1016/j.cpc.2018.05.010\n\nBoltzWann (Wannier-basis transport methodology, reimplemented in BoltzTraP.jl v0.4+):\n\nPizzi, G., Volja, D., Kozinsky, B., Fornari, M., & Marzari, N. (2014). BoltzWann: A code for the evaluation of thermoelectric and electronic transport properties with a maximally-localized Wannier functions basis. Computer Physics Communications, 185, 422-429. doi:10.1016/j.cpc.2013.09.015\n\nWannier90 v3 (MLWF construction, used via Wannier.jl):\n\nPizzi, G., et al. (2020). Wannier90 as a community code: new features and applications. Journal of Physics: Condensed Matter, 32, 165902. doi:10.1088/1361-648X/ab51ff\n\nWannier.jl (Julia MLWF construction):\n\nQiao, J. Wannier.jl: Julia package for Wannier functions [Computer software]. github.com/qiaojunfeng/Wannier.jl","title":"Citation"},{"category":"section","location":"#License","page":"Home","text":"GPL-3.0-or-later (same as original BoltzTraP2)","title":"License"},{"category":"section","location":"#Credits","page":"Home","text":"Julia port by Hiroharu Sugawara.\n\nBased on BoltzTraP2 v25.11.1 (source) by:\n\nGeorg K. H. Madsen\nJesús Carrete\nMatthieu J. Verstraete","title":"Credits"}]
}
